                "/page/23/Figure/3": ""
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "/page/23/Caption/4",
              "block_type": "Caption",
              "html": "<p>Figure 1.2: A compiler translates source code into object code, which is run by a hardware executor.</p>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  85.46484375,
                  257.748046875
                ],
                [
                  482.009765625,
                  257.748046875
                ],
                [
                  482.009765625,
                  281.0
                ],
                [
                  85.46484375,
                  281.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/22/SectionHeader/5"
              },
              "images": {}
            }
          ],
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/22/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": null
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/23/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Due to these advantages, almost all programs are written in high-level languages. Lowlevel languages are used only for a few specialized applications.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              298.93359375
            ],
            [
              482.009765625,
              298.93359375
            ],
            [
              482.009765625,
              322.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              322.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/22/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/23/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Two kinds of programs process high-level languages into low-level languages: <b>interpreters</b> and <b>compilers</b>. An interpreter reads a high-level program and executes it, meaning that it does what the program says. It processes the program a little at a time, alternately reading lines and performing computations. Figure 1.1 shows the structure of an interpreter.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.3154296875,
              330.2578125
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              330.2578125
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              378.0
            ],
            [
              85.3154296875,
              378.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/22/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/23/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>A compiler reads the program and translates it completely before the program starts running. In this context, the high-level program is called the <b>source code</b>, and the translated program is called the <b>object code</b> or the <b>executable</b>. Once a program is compiled, you can execute it repeatedly without further translation. Figure 1.2 shows the structure of a compiler.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              386.33203125
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              386.33203125
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              446.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              446.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/22/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/23/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Python is considered an interpreted language because Python programs are executed by an interpreter. There are two ways to use the interpreter: <b>interactive mode</b> and <b>script mode</b>. In interactive mode, you type Python programs and the interpreter displays the result:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              455.0
            ],
            [
              484.400390625,
              455.0
            ],
            [
              484.400390625,
              490.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              490.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/22/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/23/TextInlineMath/9",
          "block_type": "TextInlineMath",
          "html": "<p>&gt;&gt;&gt; 1 + 1</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              495.0
            ],
            [
              133.0,
              495.0
            ],
            [
              133.0,
              505.828125
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              505.828125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/22/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/23/TextInlineMath/10",
          "block_type": "TextInlineMath",
          "html": "<p>2</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.39013671875,
              507.0
            ],
            [
              92.71142578125,
              507.0
            ],
            [
              92.71142578125,
              517.4296875
            ],
            [
              85.39013671875,
              517.4296875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/22/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/23/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The chevron, &gt;&gt;&gt;, is the <b>prompt</b> the interpreter uses to indicate that it is ready. If you type 1 + 1, the interpreter replies 2.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              522.45703125
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              522.45703125
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              546.43359375
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              546.43359375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/22/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/23/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Alternatively, you can store code in a file and use the interpreter to execute the contents of the file, which is called a <b>script</b>. By convention, Python scripts have names that end with .py.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              553.0078125
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              553.0078125
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              589.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              589.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/22/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/23/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>To execute the script, you have to tell the interpreter the name of the file. If you have a script named dinsdale.py and you are working in a UNIX command window, you type python dinsdale.py. In other development environments, the details of executing scripts are different. You can find instructions for your environment at the Python website http: //python.org.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              597.09375
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              597.09375
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              657.0
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              657.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/22/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/23/Text/14",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Working in interactive mode is convenient for testing small pieces of code because you can type and execute them immediately. But for anything more than a few lines, you should save your code as a script so you can modify and execute it in the future.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              665.9296875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              665.9296875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              701.0
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              701.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/22/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        }
      ],
      "section_hierarchy": {
        "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
        "3": "/page/22/SectionHeader/5"
      },
      "images": null
    },
