        ]
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "/page/26/PageHeader/0",
          "block_type": "PageHeader",
          "html": "<p><b>1.4. Formal and natural languages 5</b></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              127.67431640625,
              61.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              61.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              71.0
            ],
            [
              127.67431640625,
              71.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/24/SectionHeader/11",
            "4": "/page/25/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/26/PageHeader/14",
          "block_type": "PageHeader",
          "html": "<p></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              520.857421875,
              61.1982421875
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              61.1982421875
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              69.99609375
            ],
            [
              520.857421875,
              69.99609375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>1.4 Formal and natural languages</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.0478515625,
              86.0
            ],
            [
              357.0,
              85.7548828125
            ],
            [
              357.0,
              100.0
            ],
            [
              128.0478515625,
              101.4169921875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/26/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Natural languages</b> are the languages people speak, such as English, Spanish, and French. They were not designed by people (although people try to impose some order on them); they evolved naturally.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.2431640625,
              114.1787109375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              114.1787109375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              149.0
            ],
            [
              129.2431640625,
              149.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/26/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Formal languages</b> are languages that are designed by people for specific applications. For example, the notation that mathematicians use is a formal language that is particularly good at denoting relationships among numbers and symbols. Chemists use a formal language to represent the chemical structure of molecules. And most importantly:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              160.0048828125
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              160.0048828125
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              208.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              208.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1",
            "4": "/page/26/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/26/SectionHeader/4",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h4><b>Programming languages are formal languages that have been designed to</b> <b>express computations.</b></h4>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              154.0458984375,
              223.330078125
            ],
            [
              502.62890625,
              223.330078125
            ],
            [
              502.62890625,
              247.0
            ],
            [
              154.0458984375,
              247.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1",
            "4": "/page/26/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/26/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Formal languages tend to have strict rules about syntax. For example, 3 + 3 = 6 is a syntactically correct mathematical statement, but 3+ = 3$6 is not. H2O is a syntactically correct chemical formula, but 2Zz is not.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.197265625,
              261.615234375
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              261.615234375
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              299.0
            ],
            [
              128.197265625,
              299.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1",
            "4": "/page/26/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/26/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Syntax rules come in two flavors, pertaining to <b>tokens</b> and structure. Tokens are the basic elements of the language, such as words, numbers, and chemical elements. One of the problems with 3+ = 3$6 is that $ is not a legal token in mathematics (at least as far as I know). Similarly, 2Zz is not legal because there is no element with the abbreviation Zz.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              309.76171875
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              309.76171875
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              358.0
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              358.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1",
            "4": "/page/26/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/26/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The second type of syntax rule pertains to the structure of a statement; that is, the way the tokens are arranged. The statement 3+ = 3 is illegal because even though + and = are legal tokens, you can't have one right after the other. Similarly, in a chemical formula the subscript comes after the element name, not before.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.5419921875,
              367.576171875
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              367.576171875
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              415.72265625
            ],
            [
              129.5419921875,
              415.72265625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1",
            "4": "/page/26/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/26/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 1.1.</b> <i>Write a well-structured English sentence with invalid tokens in it. Then write an</i><i>other sentence with all valid tokens but with invalid structure.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              415.3359375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              415.3359375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              439.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              439.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1",
            "4": "/page/26/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/26/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>When you read a sentence in English or a statement in a formal language, you have to figure out what the structure of the sentence is (although in a natural language you do this subconsciously). This process is called <b>parsing</b>.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              449.75390625
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              449.75390625
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              485.33203125
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              485.33203125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1",
            "4": "/page/26/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/26/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>For example, when you hear the sentence, \"The penny dropped,\" you understand that \"the penny\" is the subject and \"dropped\" is the predicate. Once you have parsed a sentence, you can figure out what it means, or the semantics of the sentence. Assuming that you know what a penny is and what it means to drop, you will understand the general implication of this sentence.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              496.16015625
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              496.16015625
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              556.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              556.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1",
            "4": "/page/26/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/26/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Although formal and natural languages have many features in common—tokens, structure, syntax, and semantics—there are some differences:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              566.9296875
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              566.9296875
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              590.1328125
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              590.1328125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1",
            "4": "/page/26/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/26/ListGroup/193",
          "block_type": "ListGroup",
          "html": "<p><ul><content-ref src='/page/26/ListItem/12'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/26/ListItem/13'></content-ref></ul></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.0478515625,
              606.375
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              606.375
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              701.0
            ],
            [
              128.0478515625,
              701.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "id": "/page/26/ListItem/12",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li><b>ambiguity:</b> Natural languages are full of ambiguity, which people deal with by using contextual clues and other information. Formal languages are designed to be nearly or completely unambiguous, which means that any statement has exactly one meaning, regardless of context.</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  128.0478515625,
                  606.375
                ],
                [
                  526.53515625,
                  606.375
                ],
                [
                  526.53515625,
                  654.0
                ],
                [
                  128.0478515625,
                  654.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1",
                "4": "/page/26/SectionHeader/4"
              },
              "images": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "/page/26/ListItem/13",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li><b>redundancy:</b> In order to make up for ambiguity and reduce misunderstandings, natural languages employ lots of redundancy. As a result, they are often verbose. Formal languages are less redundant and more concise.</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  129.392578125,
                  665.15625
                ],
                [
                  526.236328125,
                  665.15625
                ],
                [
                  526.236328125,
                  701.0
                ],
                [
                  129.392578125,
                  701.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1",
                "4": "/page/26/SectionHeader/4"
              },
              "images": {}
            }
          ],
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1",
            "4": "/page/26/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": null
        }
      ],
      "section_hierarchy": {
        "1": "/page/22/SectionHeader/1",
        "3": "/page/26/SectionHeader/1",
        "4": "/page/26/SectionHeader/4"
