          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/33/SectionHeader/6",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>2.2 Variables</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.83837890625,
              271.669921875
            ],
            [
              180.4921875,
              271.669921875
            ],
            [
              180.4921875,
              288.0
            ],
            [
              85.83837890625,
              288.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/32/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/33/SectionHeader/6"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/33/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>One of the most powerful features of a programming language is the ability to manipulate <b>variables</b>. A variable is a name that refers to a value.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              298.546875
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              298.546875
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              323.0
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              323.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/32/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/33/SectionHeader/6"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/33/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>An <b>assignment statement</b> creates new variables and gives them values: ''</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.3154296875,
              331.224609375
            ],
            [
              401.02734375,
              331.224609375
            ],
            [
              401.02734375,
              345.0
            ],
            [
              85.3154296875,
              345.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/32/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/33/SectionHeader/6"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/33/Code/9",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; message = And now for something completely different\n                                                            \n&gt;&gt;&gt; n = 17\n&gt;&gt;&gt; pi = 3.1415926535897932</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              347.080078125
            ],
            [
              393.2578125,
              347.080078125
            ],
            [
              393.2578125,
              383.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              383.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/32/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/33/SectionHeader/6"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/33/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>This example makes three assignments. The first assigns a string to a new variable named message; the second gives the integer 17 to n; the third assigns the (approximate) value of π to pi.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              385.751953125
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              385.751953125
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              425.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              425.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/32/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/33/SectionHeader/6"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/33/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>A common way to represent variables on paper is to write the name with an arrow pointing to the variable's value. This kind of figure is called a <b>state diagram</b> because it shows what state each of the variables is in (think of it as the variable's state of mind). Figure 2.1 shows the result of the previous example.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              431.96484375
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              431.96484375
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              481.0
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              481.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/32/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/33/SectionHeader/6"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/33/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The type of a variable is the type of the value it refers to.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.68896484375,
              489.19921875
            ],
            [
              334.6875,
              489.19921875
            ],
            [
              334.6875,
              501.0
            ],
            [
              85.68896484375,
              501.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/32/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/33/SectionHeader/6"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/33/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>&gt;&gt;&gt; type(message) &lt;type 'str'&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; type(n) &lt;type 'int'&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; type(pi) &lt;type 'float'&gt;</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              84.8671875,
              506.0
            ],
            [
              175.0,
              506.0
            ],
            [
              175.0,
              578.14453125
            ],
            [
              84.8671875,
              578.14453125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/32/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/33/SectionHeader/14"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/33/SectionHeader/14",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>2.3 Variable names and keywords</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              605.98828125
            ],
            [
              317.35546875,
              605.98828125
            ],
            [
              317.35546875,
              622.0
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              622.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/32/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/33/SectionHeader/14"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/33/Text/15",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Programmers generally choose names for their variables that are meaningful—they document what the variable is used for.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              632.671875
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              632.671875
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              656.6484375
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              656.6484375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/32/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/33/SectionHeader/14"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/33/Text/16",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Variable names can be arbitrarily long. They can contain both letters and numbers, but they have to begin with a letter. It is legal to use uppercase letters, but it is a good idea to begin variable names with a lowercase letter (you'll see why later).</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              664.3828125
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              664.3828125
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              701.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              701.0
            ]
