        },
        {
          "id": "/page/42/SectionHeader/1",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>3.4 Composition</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              86.0
            ],
            [
              247.5791015625,
              84.498046875
            ],
            [
              247.5791015625,
              100.0
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              100.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/42/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/42/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>So far, we have looked at the elements of a program—variables, expressions, and statements—in isolation, without talking about how to combine them.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              112.728515625
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              112.728515625
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              136.0
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              136.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/42/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/42/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>One of the most useful features of programming languages is their ability to take small building blocks and <b>compose</b> them. For example, the argument of a function can be any kind of expression, including arithmetic operators:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              146.0
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              144.6328125
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              181.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              181.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/42/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/42/Code/4",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>x = math.sin(degrees / 360.0 * 2 * math.pi)</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.91552734375,
              187.6552734375
            ],
            [
              355.0,
              187.6552734375
            ],
            [
              355.0,
              198.0
            ],
            [
              129.91552734375,
              198.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/42/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/42/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>And even function calls:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.09375,
              203.80078125
            ],
            [
              237.0,
              203.80078125
            ],
            [
              237.0,
              215.0
            ],
            [
              129.09375,
              215.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/42/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/42/TextInlineMath/6",
          "block_type": "TextInlineMath",
          "html": "<p>x = math.exp(math.log(x+1))</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              220.81640625
            ],
            [
              271.0,
              220.81640625
            ],
            [
              271.0,
              231.0
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              231.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/42/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/42/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Almost anywhere you can put a value, you can put an arbitrary expression, with one exception: the left side of an assignment statement has to be a variable name. Any other expression on the left side is a syntax error (we will see exceptions to this rule later).</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.392578125,
              238.025390625
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              238.025390625
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              273.0
            ],
            [
              129.392578125,
              273.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/42/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/42/Code/8",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; minutes = hours * 60 # right\n&gt;&gt;&gt; hours * 60 = minutes # wrong!\nSyntaxError: can't assign to operator</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.09375,
              280.0
            ],
            [
              386.0,
              280.0
            ],
            [
              386.0,
              314.0
            ],
            [
              127.8984375,
              314.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/42/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/42/SectionHeader/9",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>3.5 Adding new functions</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.16845703125,
              340.69921875
            ],
            [
              309.5859375,
              340.69921875
            ],
            [
              309.5859375,
              356.0
            ],
            [
              129.16845703125,
              356.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/42/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/42/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>So far, we have only been using the functions that come with Python, but it is also possible to add new functions. A <b>function definition</b> specifies the name of a new function and the sequence of statements that execute when the function is called.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              368.349609375
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              368.349609375
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              404.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              404.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/42/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/42/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Here is an example:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.0478515625,
              413.015625
            ],
            [
              216.5009765625,
              413.015625
            ],
            [
              216.5009765625,
              425.0
            ],
            [
              128.0478515625,
              425.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/42/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/42/Code/12",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>def print_lyrics():\n    print \"I'm a lumberjack, and I'm okay.\"\n    print \"I sleep all night and I work all day.\"</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.392578125,
              429.64453125
            ],
            [
              387.580078125,
              429.64453125
            ],
            [
              387.580078125,
              466.0
            ],
            [
              129.392578125,
              466.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/42/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/42/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>def is a keyword that indicates that this is a function definition. The name of the function is print_lyrics. The rules for function names are the same as for variable names: letters, numbers and some punctuation marks are legal, but the first character can't be a number. You can't use a keyword as the name of a function, and you should avoid having a variable and a function with the same name.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              471.0234375
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              471.0234375
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              532.125
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
