        {
          "id": "/page/44/Text/1",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>As you might expect, you have to create a function before you can execute it. In other words, the function definition has to be executed before the first time it is called. <b>Exercise 3.1.</b> <i>Move the last line of this program to the top, so the function call appears before the</i> <i>definitions. Run the program and see what error message you get.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              88.8486328125
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              88.8486328125
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              135.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              135.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/43/SectionHeader/12"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 3.2.</b> <i>Move the function call back to the bottom and move the definition of</i> print_lyrics <i>after the definition of</i> repeat_lyrics<i>. What happens when you run this program?</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              133.5146484375
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              133.5146484375
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              160.0
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              160.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/44/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/SectionHeader/3",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>3.7 Flow of execution</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              127.82373046875,
              188.0419921875
            ],
            [
              278.208984375,
              188.0419921875
            ],
            [
              278.208984375,
              203.0
            ],
            [
              127.82373046875,
              203.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/44/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>In order to ensure that a function is defined before its first use, you have to know the order in which statements are executed, which is called the <b>flow of execution</b>.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              214.435546875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              214.435546875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              237.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              237.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/44/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Execution always begins at the first statement of the program. Statements are executed one at a time, in order from top to bottom.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              246.33984375
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              246.33984375
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              269.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              269.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/44/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Function definitions do not alter the flow of execution of the program, but remember that statements inside the function are not executed until the function is called.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              277.857421875
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              277.857421875
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              301.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              301.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/44/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>A function call is like a detour in the flow of execution. Instead of going to the next statement, the flow jumps to the body of the function, executes all the statements there, and then comes back to pick up where it left off.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              310.921875
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              310.921875
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              346.0
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              346.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/44/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>That sounds simple enough, until you remember that one function can call another. While in the middle of one function, the program might have to execute the statements in another function. But while executing that new function, the program might have to execute yet another function!</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              354.814453125
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              354.814453125
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              402.1875
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              402.1875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/44/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Fortunately, Python is good at keeping track of where it is, so each time a function completes, the program picks up where it left off in the function that called it. When it gets to the end of the program, it terminates.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              410.6953125
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              410.6953125
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              446.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              446.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/44/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>What's the moral of this sordid tale? When you read a program, you don't always want to read from top to bottom. Sometimes it makes more sense if you follow the flow of execution.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.09375,
              456.0
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              456.0
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              491.1328125
            ],
            [
              129.09375,
              491.1328125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/44/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/SectionHeader/11",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>3.8 Parameters and arguments</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              519.75
            ],
            [
              336.0,
              519.75
            ],
            [
              336.0,
              534.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              534.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/44/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Some of the built-in functions we have seen require arguments. For example, when you call math.sin you pass a number as an argument. Some functions take more than one argument: math.pow takes two, the base and the exponent.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.2431640625,
              545.66015625
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              545.66015625
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              580.0
            ],
            [
              129.2431640625,
              580.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/44/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Inside the function, the arguments are assigned to variables called <b>parameters</b>. Here is an example of a user-defined function that takes an argument:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              589.359375
            ],
            [
              527.73046875,
              589.359375
            ],
            [
              527.73046875,
              613.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              613.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/44/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/Text/14",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>def print_twice(bruce): print bruce print bruce</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.69140625,
              618.0
            ],
            [
              250.119140625,
              618.0
            ],
            [
              250.119140625,
              655.1015625
            ],
            [
              129.69140625,
              655.1015625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/44/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/Text/15",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>This function assigns the argument to a parameter named bruce. When the function is called, it prints the value of the parameter (whatever it is) twice.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.0478515625,
              657.80859375
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              657.80859375
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              681.0
            ],
            [
              128.0478515625,
              681.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/44/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/44/Text/16",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>This function works with any value that can be printed.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.86962890625,
              689.90625
            ],
            [
              375.328125,
              689.90625
            ],
            [
