          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/48/ListGroup/188",
          "block_type": "ListGroup",
          "html": "<p><ul><content-ref src='/page/48/ListItem/1'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/48/ListItem/2'></content-ref></ul></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              143.0,
              88.8486328125
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              88.8486328125
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              143.0
            ],
            [
              143.0,
              143.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "id": "/page/48/ListItem/1",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li> Dividing a long program into functions allows you to debug the parts one at a time and then assemble them into a working whole.</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  143.0,
                  88.8486328125
                ],
                [
                  526.236328125,
                  88.8486328125
                ],
                [
                  526.236328125,
                  111.0
                ],
                [
                  143.0,
                  111.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/47/SectionHeader/17"
              },
              "images": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "/page/48/ListItem/2",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li> Well-designed functions are often useful for many programs. Once you write and debug one, you can reuse it.</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  143.0,
                  119.689453125
                ],
                [
                  526.0,
                  119.689453125
                ],
                [
                  526.0,
                  143.0
                ],
                [
                  143.0,
                  143.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/47/SectionHeader/17"
              },
              "images": {}
            }
          ],
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/48/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": null
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/48/SectionHeader/3",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>3.13 Importing with</b> from</h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              169.0
            ],
            [
              303.0,
              169.0
            ],
            [
              303.0,
              185.1416015625
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              185.1416015625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/48/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/48/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Python provides two ways to import modules; we have already seen one:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.09375,
              196.83984375
            ],
            [
              453.0,
              196.83984375
            ],
            [
              453.0,
              207.0
            ],
            [
              129.09375,
              207.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/48/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/48/Code/5",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; import math\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print math\n&lt;module 'math' (built-in)&gt;\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print math.pi\n3.14159265359</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.0478515625,
              212.0
            ],
            [
              266.1064453125,
              212.0
            ],
            [
              266.1064453125,
              280.564453125
            ],
            [
              128.0478515625,
              280.564453125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/48/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/48/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>If you import math, you get a module object named math. The module object contains constants like pi and functions like sin and exp.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.2431640625,
              276.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              275.73046875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              298.16015625
            ],
            [
              129.2431640625,
              299.70703125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/48/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/48/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>But if you try to access pi directly, you get an error.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              127.52490234375,
              307.0
            ],
            [
              355.60546875,
              307.0
            ],
            [
              355.60546875,
              317.8828125
            ],
            [
              127.52490234375,
              317.8828125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/48/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/48/Code/8",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; print pi\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"&lt;stdin&gt;\", line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nNameError: name 'pi' is not defined\nAs an alternative, you can import an object from a module like this:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; from math import pi\nNow you can access pi directly, without dot notation.\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print pi\n3.14159265359\nOr you can use the star operator to import everything from the module:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; from math import *\n&gt;&gt;&gt; cos(pi)\n-1.0</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.72021484375,
              321.75
            ],
            [
              440.0,
              321.75
            ],
            [
              440.0,
              498.0
            ],
            [
              128.72021484375,
              498.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/48/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/48/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The advantage of importing everything from the math module is that your code can be more concise. The disadvantage is that there might be conflicts between names defined in different modules, or between a name from a module and one of your variables.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              502.34765625
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              502.34765625
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              537.15234375
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              537.15234375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/48/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/48/SectionHeader/10",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>3.14 Debugging</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.42138671875,
              564.99609375
            ],
            [
              243.544921875,
              564.99609375
            ],
            [
              243.544921875,
              580.46484375
            ],
            [
              128.42138671875,
              580.46484375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/48/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/48/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>If you are using a text editor to write your scripts, you might run into problems with spaces and tabs. The best way to avoid these problems is to use spaces exclusively (no tabs). Most text editors that know about Python do this by default, but some don't.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              590.51953125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              590.51953125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              626.0
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              626.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/48/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/48/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Tabs and spaces are usually invisible, which makes them hard to debug, so try to find an editor that manages indentation for you.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              634.60546875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              634.60546875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              657.03515625
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              657.03515625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/48/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/48/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Also, don't forget to save your program before you run it. Some development environments do this automatically, but some don't. In that case the program you are looking at in the text editor is not the same as the program you are running.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              664.76953125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              664.76953125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              701.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              701.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/48/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        }
      ],
      "section_hierarchy": {
        "1": "/page/40/SectionHeader/1",
        "3": "/page/48/SectionHeader/10"
      },
      "images": null
