        },
        {
          "id": "/page/62/SectionHeader/2",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>5.1 Modulus operator</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.42138671875,
              287.33203125
            ],
            [
              280.001953125,
              287.33203125
            ],
            [
              280.001953125,
              302.0
            ],
            [
              128.42138671875,
              302.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/62/SectionHeader/2"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/62/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The <b>modulus operator</b> works on integers and yields the remainder when the first operand is divided by the second. In Python, the modulus operator is a percent sign (%). The syntax is the same as for other operators:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              312.46875
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              312.46875
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              347.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              347.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/62/SectionHeader/2"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/62/Code/4",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; quotient = 7 / 3\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print quotient\n2\n&gt;&gt;&gt; remainder = 7 % 3\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print remainder\n1\nSo 7 divided by 3 is 2 with 1 left over.</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.5419921875,
              351.333984375
            ],
            [
              294.0,
              351.333984375
            ],
            [
              294.0,
              437.0
            ],
            [
              129.5419921875,
              437.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/62/SectionHeader/2"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/62/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The modulus operator turns out to be surprisingly useful. For example, you can check whether one number is divisible by another—if x % y is zero, then x is divisible by y.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              127.8984375,
              443.1796875
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              443.1796875
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              468.0
            ],
            [
              127.8984375,
              468.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/62/SectionHeader/2"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/62/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Also, you can extract the right-most digit or digits from a number. For example, x % 10 yields the right-most digit of x (in base 10). Similarly x % 100 yields the last two digits.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.09375,
              475.27734375
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              475.27734375
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              498.48046875
            ],
            [
              129.09375,
              498.48046875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/62/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/62/SectionHeader/7",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>5.2 Boolean expressions</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.2431640625,
              523.6171875
            ],
            [
              295.6904296875,
              523.6171875
            ],
            [
              295.6904296875,
              539.0
            ],
            [
              129.2431640625,
              539.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/62/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/62/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>A <b>boolean expression</b> is an expression that is either true or false. The following examples use the operator ==, which compares two operands and produces True if they are equal and False otherwise:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              549.9140625
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              549.9140625
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              584.0
            ],
