          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/63/Text/1",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The == operator is one of the <b>relational operators</b>; the others are:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              89.0
            ],
            [
              370.0,
              88.365234375
            ],
            [
              370.0,
              99.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              100.16015625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/62/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/63/Table/2",
          "block_type": "Table",
          "html": "<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th>x != y  </th><th># x is not equal to y            </th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>x &gt; y   </td><td># x is greater than y            </td></tr>\n<tr><td>x &lt; y   </td><td># x is less than y               </td></tr>\n<tr><td>x &gt;= y  </td><td># x is greater than or equal to y</td></tr>\n<tr><td>x &lt;= y  </td><td># x is less than or equal to y   </td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              115.87060546875,
              101.126953125
            ],
            [
              400.0,
              101.126953125
            ],
            [
              400.0,
              162.0
            ],
            [
              115.87060546875,
              162.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/62/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/63/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Although these operations are probably familiar to you, the Python symbols are different from the mathematical symbols. A common error is to use a single equal sign (=) instead of a double equal sign (==). Remember that = is an assignment operator and == is a relational operator. There is no such thing as =&lt; or =&gt;.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              166.3857421875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              166.3857421875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              213.08203125
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              213.08203125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/63/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/63/SectionHeader/4",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>5.3 Logical operators</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              239.958984375
            ],
            [
              231.0,
              239.958984375
            ],
            [
              231.0,
              255.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              255.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/63/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/63/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>There are three <b>logical operators</b>: and, or, and not. The semantics (meaning) of these operators is similar to their meaning in English. For example, x &gt; 0 and x &lt; 10 is true only if x is greater than 0 and less than 10.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              264.515625
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              264.515625
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              300.0
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              300.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/63/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/63/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>n%2 == 0 or n%3 == 0 is true if <i>either</i> of the conditions is true, that is, if the number is divisible by 2 or 3.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              307.248046875
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              307.248046875
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              331.0
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              331.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/63/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/63/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Finally, the not operator negates a boolean expression, so not (x &gt; y) is true if x &gt; y is false, that is, if x is less than or equal to y.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              338.765625
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              338.765625
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              361.1953125
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              361.1953125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/63/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/63/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Strictly speaking, the operands of the logical operators should be boolean expressions, but Python is not very strict. Any nonzero number is interpreted as \"true.\"</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              369.31640625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              369.31640625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              392.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              392.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/63/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/63/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>&gt;&gt;&gt; 17 and True</p>",
          "polygon": [
