        },
        {
          "id": "/page/66/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>A function that calls itself is <b>recursive</b>; the process is called <b>recursion</b>.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              220.81640625
            ],
            [
              436.0,
              220.81640625
            ],
            [
              436.0,
              232.41796875
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              232.41796875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/65/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/66/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>As another example, we can write a function that prints a string n times.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              241.892578125
            ],
            [
              447.0,
              241.892578125
            ],
            [
              447.0,
              253.107421875
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              253.107421875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/65/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/66/TextInlineMath/11",
          "block_type": "TextInlineMath",
          "html": "<p>def print_n(s, n): if n &lt;= 0: return print s print_n(s, n-1)</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              127.30078125,
              258.908203125
            ],
            [
              229.0,
              258.908203125
            ],
            [
              229.0,
              319.236328125
            ],
            [
              127.30078125,
              319.236328125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/65/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/66/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>If n &lt;= 0 the return statement exits the function. The flow of execution immediately returns to the caller, and the remaining lines of the function are not executed.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.09375,
              324.650390625
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              324.650390625
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              348.240234375
            ],
            [
              129.09375,
              348.240234375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/65/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/66/TextInlineMath/13",
          "block_type": "TextInlineMath",
          "html": "<p>The rest of the function is similar to countdown: if n is greater than 0, it displays s and then calls itself to display s n − 1 additional times. So the number of lines of output is 1 + (n - 1), which adds up to n.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              355.974609375
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              355.974609375
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              392.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              392.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/65/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/66/Text/14",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>For simple examples like this, it is probably easier to use a for loop. But we will see examples later that are hard to write with a for loop and easy to write with recursion, so it is good to start early.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              401.80078125
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              401.80078125
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              437.37890625
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              437.37890625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/66/SectionHeader/15"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/66/SectionHeader/15",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>5.9 Stack diagrams for recursive functions</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              465.99609375
            ],
            [
              414.7734375,
              465.99609375
            ],
            [
              414.7734375,
              482.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              482.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/66/SectionHeader/15"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/66/Text/16",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>In Section 3.10, we used a stack diagram to represent the state of a program during a function call. The same kind of diagram can help interpret a recursive function.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              493.453125
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              493.453125
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              518.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              518.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/66/SectionHeader/15"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/66/Text/17",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Every time a function gets called, Python creates a new function frame, which contains the function's local variables and parameters. For a recursive function, there might be more than one frame on the stack at the same time.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              527.09765625
            ],
            [
              527.431640625,
              527.09765625
            ],
            [
              527.431640625,
              563.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              563.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/66/SectionHeader/15"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/66/Text/18",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Figure 5.1 shows a stack diagram for countdown called with n = 3.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              572.34375
            ],
            [
              424.3359375,
              572.34375
            ],
            [
              424.3359375,
              584.0
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              584.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/66/SectionHeader/15"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/66/Text/19",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>As usual, the top of the stack is the frame for __main__. It is empty because we did not create any variables in __main__ or pass any arguments to it.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              592.83984375
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              592.83984375
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              616.04296875
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              616.04296875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/66/SectionHeader/15"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/66/Text/20",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The four countdown frames have different values for the parameter n. The bottom of the stack, where n=0, is called the <b>base case</b>. It does not make a recursive call, so there are no more frames. ''</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.197265625,
              626.09765625
            ],
            [
              527.431640625,
              626.09765625
            ],
            [
              527.431640625,
              662.0
            ],
            [
              128.197265625,
              662.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/62/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/66/SectionHeader/15"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/66/Text/21",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 5.1.</b> <i>Draw a stack diagram for</i> print_n <i>called with</i> s = Helloand n=2.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              663.22265625
            ],
