          },
          "images": null
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/78/SectionHeader/3",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>6.6 Leap of faith</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.197265625,
              250.98046875
            ],
            [
              246.0,
              250.98046875
            ],
            [
              246.0,
              266.0
            ],
            [
              128.197265625,
              266.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/78/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Following the flow of execution is one way to read programs, but it can quickly become labyrinthine. An alternative is what I call the \"leap of faith.\" When you come to a function call, instead of following the flow of execution, you <i>assume</i> that the function works correctly and returns the right result.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              278.82421875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              278.82421875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              326.390625
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              326.390625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/78/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>In fact, you are already practicing this leap of faith when you use built-in functions. When you call math.cos or math.exp, you don't examine the bodies of those functions. You just assume that they work because the people who wrote the built-in functions were good programmers.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              336.251953125
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              336.251953125
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              383.0
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              383.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/78/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The same is true when you call one of your own functions. For example, in Section 6.4, we wrote a function called is_divisible that determines whether one number is divisible by another. Once we have convinced ourselves that this function is correct—by examining the code and testing—we can use the function without looking at the body again.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              393.6796875
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              393.6796875
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              441.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              441.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/78/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The same is true of recursive programs. When you get to the recursive call, instead of following the flow of execution, you should assume that the recursive call works (yields the correct result) and then ask yourself, \"Assuming that I can find the factorial of n − 1, can I compute the factorial of n?\" In this case, it is clear that you can, by multiplying by n.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              451.30078125
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              451.30078125
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              498.48046875
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              498.48046875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/78/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Of course, it's a bit strange to assume that the function works correctly when you haven't finished writing it, but that's why it's called a leap of faith!</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.09375,
              508.921875
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              508.921875
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              532.0
            ],
            [
              129.09375,
              532.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/78/SectionHeader/9",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>6.7 One more example</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.197265625,
              562.67578125
            ],
            [
              285.978515625,
              562.67578125
            ],
            [
              285.978515625,
              578.0
            ],
            [
              128.197265625,
              578.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/78/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>After factorial, the most common example of a recursively defined mathematical function is fibonacci, which has the following definition (see http://en.wikipedia.org/ wiki/Fibonacci_number):</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              590.90625
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              590.90625
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              626.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              626.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/78/Equation/11",
          "block_type": "Equation",
          "html": "\n<p><math>fibonacci(0) = 0 fibonacci(1) = 1 fibonacci(n) = fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2)</math></p>\n",
          "polygon": [
            [
              226.0634765625,
              637.0
            ],
            [
              448.83984375,
              637.0
            ],
            [
              448.83984375,
              679.8515625
            ],
            [
              226.0634765625,
              679.8515625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/78/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Translated into Python, it looks like this:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              130.0,
              690.29296875
            ],
            [
              307.0,
              690.29296875
            ],
            [
              307.0,
              701.0
            ],
            [
              130.0,
              701.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        }
      ],
      "section_hierarchy": {
        "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
        "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/9"
      },
      "images": null
    },
    {
      "id": "/page/79/Page/214",
      "block_type": "Page",
      "html": "<content-ref src='/page/79/PageHeader/0'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/79/PageHeader/13'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/79/Text/14'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/79/Code/1'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/79/Text/2'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/79/SectionHeader/3'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/79/Text/4'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/79/Code/5'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/79/Text/6'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/79/Text/7'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/79/Text/8'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/79/Text/9'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/79/Code/10'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/79/Text/11'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/79/Code/12'></content-ref>",
      "polygon": [
        [
          0.0,
          0.0
        ],
        [
          612.0,
          0.0
        ],
        [
          612.0,
          792.0
        ],
        [
          0.0,
          792.0
        ]
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "/page/79/PageHeader/0",
          "block_type": "PageHeader",
          "html": "<p><b>58 Chapter 6. Fruitful functions</b></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              59.94140625
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              59.94140625
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              71.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              71.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/79/PageHeader/13",
          "block_type": "PageHeader",
          "html": "<p></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              59.8447265625
            ],
            [
              95.923828125,
              59.8447265625
            ],
            [
              95.923828125,
              69.3193359375
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              69.3193359375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/79/Text/14",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p> </p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              416.0,
              521.0
            ],
            [
              417.0,
              521.0
            ],
            [
              417.0,
              522.0
            ],
            [
              416.0,
              522.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/79/Code/1",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>def fibonacci (n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    elif n == 1:\n        return 1\n    else:\n        return fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2)</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              88.89697265625
            ],
            [
              327.0,
              88.89697265625
            ],
            [
              327.0,
              172.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              172.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/78/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/79/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>If you try to follow the flow of execution here, even for fairly small values of n, your head explodes. But according to the leap of faith, if you assume that the two recursive calls work correctly, then it is clear that you get the right result by adding them together.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.0166015625,
              174.1201171875
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              174.1201171875
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              212.0
            ],
            [
              85.0166015625,
              212.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/79/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/79/SectionHeader/3",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>6.8 Checking types</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.3154296875,
              237.251953125
            ],
            [
              220.0,
              237.251953125
            ],
            [
              220.0,
              254.0
            ],
            [
              85.3154296875,
              254.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/79/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/79/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>What happens if we call factorial and give it 1.5 as an argument?</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.83837890625,
              263.162109375
            ],
            [
              381.3046875,
              263.162109375
            ],
            [
              381.3046875,
              275.0
            ],
            [
              85.83837890625,
              275.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/79/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/79/Code/5",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; factorial(1.5)\nRuntimeError: Maximum recursion depth exceeded</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              84.568359375,
              277.083984375
            ],
            [
              335.8828125,
              277.083984375
            ],
            [
              335.8828125,
              302.0
            ],
            [
              84.568359375,
              302.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/79/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/79/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>It looks like an infinite recursion. But how can that be? There is a base case—when n == 0. But if n is not an integer, we can <i>miss</i> the base case and recurse forever.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              305.89453125
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              305.89453125
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              330.0
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              330.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/79/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/79/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>In the first recursive call, the value of n is 0.5. In the next, it is -0.5. From there, it gets smaller (more negative), but it will never be 0.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              336.638671875
            ],
            [
              482.607421875,
              336.638671875
            ],
            [
              482.607421875,
              361.0
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              361.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/79/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/79/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>We have two choices. We can try to generalize the factorial function to work with floating-point numbers, or we can make factorial check the type of its argument. The first option is called the gamma function and it's a little beyond the scope of this book. So we'll go for the second.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              368.15625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              368.15625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              417.26953125
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              417.26953125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/79/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/79/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>We can use the built-in function isinstance to verify the type of the argument. While we're at it, we can also make sure the argument is positive:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              423.84375
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              423.84375
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              448.0
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              448.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/72/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/79/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/79/Code/10",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>def factorial (n):\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        print 'Factorial is only defined for integers.'\n                                                          \n        return None\n    elif n &lt; 0:\n        print 'Factorial is not defined for negative integers.'return None\n    elif n == 0:\n        return 1\n    else:\n        return n * factorial(n-1)</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              84.568359375,
              453.0
            ],
            [
              416.0,
              453.0
