        {
          "id": "/page/85/SectionHeader/3",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>7.2 Updating variables</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              163.58203125
            ],
            [
              244.0,
              163.58203125
            ],
            [
              244.0,
              179.05078125
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              179.05078125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/84/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/85/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/85/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>One of the most common forms of multiple assignment is an <b>update</b>, where the new value of the variable depends on the old.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              189.4921875
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              189.4921875
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              214.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              214.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/84/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/85/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/85/TextInlineMath/5",
          "block_type": "TextInlineMath",
          "html": "<p>x = x+1</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              219.462890625
            ],
            [
              124.611328125,
              219.462890625
            ],
            [
              124.611328125,
              230.291015625
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              230.291015625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/84/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/85/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/85/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>This means \"get the current value of x, add one, and then update x with the new value.\"</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              235.51171875
            ],
            [
              475.13671875,
              235.51171875
            ],
            [
              475.13671875,
              246.7265625
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              246.7265625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/84/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/85/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/85/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>If you try to update a variable that doesn't exist, you get an error, because Python evaluates the right side before it assigns a value to x:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              255.041015625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              255.041015625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              279.0
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              279.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/84/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/85/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/85/Code/8",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = x+1\nNameError: name 'x' is not defined</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              284.0
            ],
            [
              264.0,
              284.0
            ],
            [
              264.0,
              310.1484375
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              310.1484375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/84/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/85/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/85/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Before you can update a variable, you have to <b>initialize</b> it, usually with a simple assignment:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              313.048828125
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              313.048828125
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              336.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              336.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/84/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/85/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/85/TextInlineMath/10",
          "block_type": "TextInlineMath",
          "html": "<p>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = 0 &gt;&gt;&gt; x = x+1</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              342.0
            ],
            [
              144.0,
              342.0
            ],
            [
              144.0,
              365.44921875
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              365.44921875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/84/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/85/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/85/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Updating a variable by adding 1 is called an <b>increment</b>; subtracting 1 is called a <b>decrement</b>.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              368.15625
            ],
            [
              482.607421875,
              368.15625
            ],
            [
              482.607421875,
              381.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              381.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/84/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/85/SectionHeader/12"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/85/SectionHeader/12",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>7.3 The</b> while <b>statement</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              420.75
            ],
            [
              252.2109375,
              420.75
            ],
            [
              252.2109375,
              438.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              438.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/84/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/85/SectionHeader/12"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/85/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Computers are often used to automate repetitive tasks. Repeating identical or similar tasks without making errors is something that computers do well and people do poorly.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              448.59375
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              448.59375
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              473.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              473.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/84/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/85/SectionHeader/12"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/85/Text/14",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>We have seen two programs, countdown and print_n, that use recursion to perform repetition, which is also called <b>iteration</b>. Because iteration is so common, Python provides several language features to make it easier. One is the for statement we saw in Section 4.2. We'll get back to that later.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              482.625
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              482.625
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              530.0
