          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/Text/1",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>An empty string contains no characters and has length 0, but other than that, it is the same as any other string.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              88.22021484375
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              88.22021484375
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              111.0
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              111.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/94/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 8.3.</b> <i>Given that</i> fruit <i>is a string, what does</i> fruit[:] <i>mean?</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              112.1484375
            ],
            [
              379.0,
              112.1484375
            ],
            [
              379.0,
              123.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              123.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/SectionHeader/3",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>8.5 Strings are immutable</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.24072265625,
              150.43359375
            ],
            [
              265.0,
              150.43359375
            ],
            [
              265.0,
              165.0
            ],
            [
              85.24072265625,
              165.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>It is tempting to use the [] operator on the left side of an assignment, with the intention of changing a character in a string. For example: ''</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              175.376953125
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              175.376953125
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              200.0
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              200.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/TextInlineMath/5",
          "block_type": "TextInlineMath",
          "html": "<p>&gt;&gt;&gt; greeting = Hello, world!  &gt;&gt;&gt; greeting[0] = 'J'  ''</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.83837890625,
              203.0
            ],
            [
              244.740234375,
              202.060546875
            ],
            [
              244.740234375,
              226.0
            ],
            [
              85.83837890625,
              226.810546875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>TypeError: strobject does not support item assignment</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              228.0
            ],
            [
              380.109375,
              228.0
            ],
            [
              380.109375,
              238.798828125
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              238.798828125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The \"object\" in this case is the string and the \"item\" is the character you tried to assign. For now, an <b>object</b> is the same thing as a value, but we will refine that definition later. An <b>item</b> is one of the values in a sequence.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              242.859375
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              242.859375
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              278.05078125
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              278.05078125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The reason for the error is that strings are <b>immutable</b>, which means you can't change an existing string. The best you can do is create a new string that is a variation on the original: ''</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              286.365234375
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              286.365234375
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              311.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              311.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/TextInlineMath/9",
          "block_type": "TextInlineMath",
          "html": "<p>&gt;&gt;&gt; greeting = Hello, world!  &gt;&gt;&gt; new_greeting = 'J' + greeting[1:] &gt;&gt;&gt; print new_greeting Jello, world!</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              313.0
            ],
            [
              280.0,
              313.0
            ],
            [
              280.0,
              361.775390625
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              361.775390625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>This example concatenates a new first letter onto a slice of greeting. It has no effect on the original string.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              364.67578125
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              364.67578125
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              388.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              388.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/SectionHeader/11",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>8.6 Searching</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              415.3359375
            ],
            [
              184.0,
              415.3359375
            ],
            [
              184.0,
              431.578125
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              431.578125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>What does the following function do?</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              84.79248046875,
              442.0
            ],
            [
              253.0,
              442.0
            ],
            [
              253.0,
              452.84765625
            ],
            [
              84.79248046875,
              452.84765625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/Code/13",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>def find(word, letter):\n    index = 0\n    while index &lt; len(word):\n        if word[index] == letter:\n            return index\n        index = index + 1\n    return -1</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              454.78125
            ],
            [
              259.0,
              454.78125
            ],
            [
              259.0,
              542.1796875
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              542.1796875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/Text/14",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>In a sense, find is the opposite of the [] operator. Instead of taking an index and extracting the corresponding character, it takes a character and finds the index where that character appears. If the character is not found, the function returns -1.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              544.5
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              544.5
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              580.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              580.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/Text/15",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>This is the first example we have seen of a return statement inside a loop. If word[index] == letter, the function breaks out of the loop and returns immediately.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              587.8125
            ],
            [
              482.0,
              587.8125
            ],
            [
              482.0,
              611.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              611.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/Text/16",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>If the character doesn't appear in the string, the program exits the loop normally and returns -1.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              618.36328125
            ],
            [
              482.0,
              618.36328125
            ],
            [
              482.0,
              642.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              642.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/Text/17",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>This pattern of computation—traversing a sequence and returning when we find what we are looking for—is called a <b>search</b>.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              649.6875
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              649.6875
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              674.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              674.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/95/Text/18",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 8.4.</b> <i>Modify</i> find <i>so that it has a third parameter, the index in</i> word <i>where it should start</i> <i>looking.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              674.82421875
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              674.82421875
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              698.0
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              698.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        }
      ],
      "section_hierarchy": {
        "1": "/page/92/SectionHeader/1",
        "3": "/page/95/SectionHeader/11"
