          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/102/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/107/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/107/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 9.7.</b> <i>This question is based on a Puzzler that was broadcast on the radio program</i> Car Talk (http: // www. cartalk. com/ content/ puzzlers ):</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              111.6650390625
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              110.1181640625
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              134.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              134.6748046875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/102/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/107/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/107/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>Give me a word with three consecutive double letters. I'll give you a couple of words</i> <i>that almost qualify, but don't. For example, the word committee, c-o-m-m-i-t-t-e-e. It</i> <i>would be great except for the 'i' that sneaks in there. Or Mississippi: M-i-s-s-i-s-s-i</i><i>p-p-i. If you could take out those i's it would work. But there is a word that has three</i> <i>consecutive pairs of letters and to the best of my knowledge this may be the only word.</i> <i>Of course there are probably 500 more but I can only think of one. What is the word?</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              110.1181640625,
              147.7265625
            ],
            [
              457.8046875,
              147.7265625
            ],
            [
              457.8046875,
              219.462890625
            ],
            [
              110.1181640625,
              219.462890625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/102/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/107/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/107/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>Write a program to find it. Solution:</i> http: // thinkpython. com/ code/ cartalk1. py . <b>Exercise 9.8.</b> <i>Here's another</i> Car Talk <i>Puzzler (</i>http: // www. cartalk. com/ content/ puzzlers ):</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              232.8046875
            ],
            [
              481.11328125,
              232.8046875
            ],
            [
              481.11328125,
              268.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              268.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/102/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/107/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/107/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>\"I was driving on the highway the other day and I happened to notice my odometer.</i> <i>Like most odometers, it shows six digits, in whole miles only. So, if my car had 300,000</i> <i>miles, for example, I'd see 3-0-0-0-0-0.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              110.865234375,
              280.37109375
            ],
            [
              458.103515625,
              280.37109375
            ],
            [
              458.103515625,
              316.0
            ],
            [
              110.865234375,
              316.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/102/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/107/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/107/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>\"Now, what I saw that day was very interesting. I noticed that the last 4 digits were</i> <i>palindromic; that is, they read the same forward as backward. For example, 5-4-4-5 is a</i> <i>palindrome, so my odometer could have read 3-1-5-4-4-5.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              109.96875,
              321.943359375
            ],
            [
              458.40234375,
              321.943359375
            ],
            [
              458.40234375,
              357.521484375
            ],
            [
              109.96875,
              357.521484375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/102/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/107/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/107/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>\"One mile later, the last 5 numbers were palindromic. For example, it could have read</i> <i>3-6-5-4-5-6. One mile after that, the middle 4 out of 6 numbers were palindromic. And</i> <i>you ready for this? One mile later, all 6 were palindromic!</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              110.267578125,
              361.96875
            ],
            [
              457.505859375,
              361.96875
            ],
            [
              457.505859375,
              398.0
            ],
            [
              110.267578125,
              398.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/102/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/107/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/107/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>\"The question is, what was on the odometer when I first looked?\"</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              111.0,
              402.57421875
            ],
            [
              372.638671875,
              402.57421875
            ],
            [
              372.638671875,
              414.0
            ],
            [
              111.0,
              414.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/102/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/107/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/107/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>Write a Python program that tests all the six-digit numbers and prints any numbers that satisfy</i> <i>these requirements. Solution:</i> http: // thinkpython. com/ code/ cartalk2. py . <b>Exercise 9.9.</b> <i>Here's another</i> Car Talk <i>Puzzler you can solve with a search (</i>http: // www. cartalk. com/ content/ puzzlers ):</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              427.32421875
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              427.32421875
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              474.0
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              474.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/102/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/107/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/107/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>\"Recently I had a visit with my mom and we realized that the two digits that make</i> <i>up my age when reversed resulted in her age. For example, if she's 73, I'm 37. We</i> <i>wondered how often this has happened over the years but we got sidetracked with other</i> <i>topics and we never came up with an answer.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              110.865234375,
              486.87890625
            ],
            [
              458.103515625,
              486.87890625
            ],
            [
              458.103515625,
              535.0
            ],
            [
              110.865234375,
              535.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/102/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/107/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/107/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>\"When I got home I figured out that the digits of our ages have been reversible six times</i> <i>so far. I also figured out that if we're lucky it would happen again in a few years, and</i> <i>if we're really lucky it would happen one more time after that. In other words, it would</i> <i>have happened 8 times over all. So the question is, how old am I now?\"</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              111.0,
              541.0
            ],
            [
              459.59765625,
              541.0
            ],
            [
              459.59765625,
              588.0
            ],
            [
              111.0,
              588.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/102/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/107/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/107/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>Write a Python program that searches for solutions to this Puzzler. Hint: you might find the string</i> <i>method</i> zfill <i>useful.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              601.34765625
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              601.34765625
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
