        },
        {
          "id": "/page/118/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Part of the problem with lists is that there are too many ways to do things. For example, to remove an element from a list, you can use pop, remove, del, or even a slice assignment.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              153.7470703125,
              104.1240234375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              104.1240234375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              139.3154296875
            ],
            [
              153.7470703125,
              139.3154296875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/108/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/117/SectionHeader/12"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/118/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>To add an element, you can use the append method or the + operator. Assuming that t is a list and x is a list element, these are right:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              154.79296875,
              144.3427734375
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              144.3427734375
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              167.0
            ],
            [
              154.79296875,
              167.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/108/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/117/SectionHeader/12"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/118/Code/4",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>t.append(x)\nt = t + [x]</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
