        },
        {
          "id": "/page/126/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The result when you raise an exception is the same as when Python raises one: it prints a traceback and an error message.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              154.5908203125
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              154.5908203125
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              177.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              177.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/125/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/126/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The raise statement takes a detailed error message as an optional argument. For example: ''</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.197265625,
              187.365234375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              187.365234375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              202.0
            ],
            [
              128.197265625,
              202.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/125/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/126/Code/4",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; raise ValueError(value does not appear in the dictionary)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n File \"&lt;stdin&gt;\", line 1, in ?\nValueError: value does not appear in the dictionary</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              127.599609375,
              205.0
            ],
            [
              459.0,
              205.0
            ],
            [
              459.0,
              253.107421875
            ],
            [
              127.599609375,
              253.107421875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/125/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/126/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>A reverse lookup is much slower than a forward lookup; if you have to do it often, or if the dictionary gets big, the performance of your program will suffer.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              258.71484375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              258.71484375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              281.14453125
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              281.14453125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/125/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/126/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 11.4.</b> <i>Modify</i> reverse_lookup <i>so that it builds and returns a list of</i> all <i>keys that map to</i> v<i>, or an empty list if there are none.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              283.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              283.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              305.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              305.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>11.4 Dictionaries and lists</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              127.8984375,
              337.21875
            ],
            [
              308.390625,
              337.21875
            ],
            [
              308.390625,
              352.0
            ],
            [
              127.8984375,
              352.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/126/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Lists can appear as values in a dictionary. For example, if you were given a dictionary that maps from letters to frequencies, you might want to invert it; that is, create a dictionary that maps from frequencies to letters. Since there might be several letters with the same frequency, each value in the inverted dictionary should be a list of letters.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              364.67578125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              364.67578125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              412.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              412.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/126/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Here is a function that inverts a dictionary:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.2431640625,
              422.68359375
            ],
            [
              318.0,
              422.68359375
            ],
            [
              318.0,
              433.0
            ],
            [
              129.2431640625,
              433.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/126/Code/10",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>def invert_dict(d):\n    inverse = dict()\n    for key in d:\n        val = d[key]\n        if val not in inverse:\n            inverse[val] = [key]\n        else:\n            inverse[val].append(key)\n    return inverse</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.91552734375,
              440.0
            ],
            [
              318.55078125,
              440.0
            ],
            [
              318.55078125,
              548.3671875
            ],
            [
              129.91552734375,
              548.3671875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/126/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Each time through the loop, key gets a key from d and val gets the corresponding value. If val is not in inverse, that means we haven't seen it before, so we create a new item and initialize it with a <b>singleton</b> (a list that contains a single element). Otherwise we have seen this value before, so we append the corresponding key to the list.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              555.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              555.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              602.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              602.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/126/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Here is an example:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              612.5625
            ],
            [
              216.3515625,
              612.5625
            ],
            [
              216.0,
              623.0
            ],
            [
              127.599609375,
              623.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/126/Code/13",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; hist = histogram(parrot)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print hist\n{'a': 1, 'p': 1, 'r': 2, 't': 1, 'o': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; inverse = invert_dict(hist)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print inverse\n{1: ['a', 'p', 't', 'o'], 2: ['r']}</pre>",
          "polygon": [
