          },
          "images": null
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/127/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Figure 11.1 is a state diagram showing hist and inverse. A dictionary is represented as a box with the type dict above it and the key-value pairs inside. If the values are integers, floats or strings, I usually draw them inside the box, but I usually draw lists outside the box, just to keep the diagram simple.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              238.9921875
            ],
            [
              482.607421875,
              238.9921875
            ],
            [
              482.607421875,
              286.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              286.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/127/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Lists can be values in a dictionary, as this example shows, but they cannot be keys. Here's what happens if you try:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              293.326171875
            ],
            [
              482.607421875,
              293.326171875
            ],
            [
              482.607421875,
              317.0
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              317.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/127/TextInlineMath/5",
          "block_type": "TextInlineMath",
          "html": "<p>&gt;&gt;&gt; t = [1, 2, 3] &gt;&gt;&gt; d = dict() &gt;&gt;&gt; d[t] = 'oops'  Traceback (most recent call last): File \"&lt;stdin&gt;\", line 1, in ? TypeError: list objects are unhashable</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              320.396484375
            ],
            [
              285.0,
              320.396484375
            ],
            [
              285.0,
              392.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              392.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/127/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>I mentioned earlier that a dictionary is implemented using a hashtable and that means that the keys have to be <b>hashable</b>.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              394.06640625
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              394.06640625
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              419.0
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              419.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/127/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>A <b>hash</b> is a function that takes a value (of any kind) and returns an integer. Dictionaries use these integers, called hash values, to store and look up key-value pairs.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              425.77734375
            ],
            [
              482.607421875,
              425.77734375
            ],
            [
              482.607421875,
              449.0
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              449.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/127/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>This system works fine if the keys are immutable. But if the keys are mutable, like lists, bad things happen. For example, when you create a key-value pair, Python hashes the key and stores it in the corresponding location. If you modify the key and then hash it again, it would go to a different location. In that case you might have two entries for the same key, or you might not be able to find a key. Either way, the dictionary wouldn't work correctly.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              457.48828125
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              457.48828125
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              517.81640625
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              517.81640625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/127/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>That's why the keys have to be hashable, and why mutable types like lists aren't. The simplest way to get around this limitation is to use tuples, which we will see in the next chapter.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              523.6171875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              523.6171875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              559.1953125
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              559.1953125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/127/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Since lists and dictionaries are mutable, they can't be used as keys, but they can be used as values.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              566.9296875
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              566.9296875
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              590.0
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              590.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/126/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/127/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 11.5.</b> <i>Read the documentation of the dictionary method</i> setdefault <i>and use it to write a</i> <i>more concise version of</i> invert_dict<i>. Solution:</i> http: // thinkpython. com/ code/ invert_ dict. py .</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              592.0
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              590.90625
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              627.0
