        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>11.6 Global variables</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              84.7880859375
            ],
            [
              235.0,
              83.2412109375
            ],
            [
              235.0,
              100.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              100.4501953125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>In the previous example, known is created outside the function, so it belongs to the special frame called __main__. Variables in __main__ are sometimes called <b>global</b> because they can be accessed from any function. Unlike local variables, which disappear when their function ends, global variables persist from one function call to the next.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              110.1181640625
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              110.1181640625
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              159.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              159.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>It is common to use global variables for <b>flags</b>; that is, boolean variables that indicate (\"flag\") whether a condition is true. For example, some programs use a flag named verbose to control the level of detail in the output:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              166.1923828125
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              166.1923828125
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              203.02734375
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              203.02734375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/Text/14",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>' </p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              249.0,
              244.0
            ],
            [
              255.0,
              244.0
            ],
            [
              255.0,
              266.0
            ],
            [
              249.0,
              266.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/Code/4",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>verbose = True\ndef example1():\n    if verbose:\n         print 'Running example1If you try to reassign a global variable, you might be surprised. The following example is\nsupposed to keep track of whether the function has been called:\nbeen_called = False</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              209.0
            ],
            [
              482.0,
              209.0
            ],
            [
              482.0,
              328.517578125
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              328.517578125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>def example2(): been_called = True # WRONG</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              326.0
            ],
            [
              285.0,
              326.0
            ],
            [
              285.0,
              348.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              348.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>But if you run it you will see that the value of been_called doesn't change. The problem is that example2 creates a new local variable named been_called. The local variable goes away when the function ends, and has no effect on the global variable.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              350.3671875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              350.3671875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              388.265625
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              388.265625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>To reassign a global variable inside a function you have to <b>declare</b> the global variable before you use it:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              395.033203125
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              395.033203125
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              420.0
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              420.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/Code/8",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>been_called = False\ndef example2():\n    global been_called\n    been_called = True</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.0166015625,
              426.0
            ],
            [
              201.0,
              426.0
            ],
            [
              201.0,
              485.0
            ],
            [
              85.0166015625,
              485.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The global statement tells the interpreter something like, \"In this function, when I say been_called, I mean the global variable; don't create a local one.\"</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              489.19921875
            ],
            [
              482.009765625,
              489.19921875
            ],
            [
              482.009765625,
              513.17578125
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              513.17578125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/Text/15",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>'</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              296.0,
              593.0
            ],
            [
              301.0,
              593.0
            ],
            [
              301.0,
              603.0
            ],
            [
              296.0,
              603.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/Text/16",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>referenced before assignment</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              301.0,
              607.0
            ],
            [
              452.0,
              607.0
            ],
            [
              452.0,
              617.0
            ],
            [
              301.0,
              617.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Here's an example that tries to update a global variable:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              520.5234375
            ],
            [
              333.193359375,
              520.5234375
            ],
            [
              333.193359375,
              533.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              533.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/Code/11",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>count = 0\ndef example3():\n    count = count + 1 # WRONG\nIf you run it you get:\nUnboundLocalError: local variable 'countPython assumes that count is local, which means that you are reading it before writing it.\nThe solution, again, is to declare count global.\ndef example3():\n    global count\n    count += 1</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              84.568359375,
              538.0
            ],
            [
              482.0,
              538.0
            ],
            [
              482.0,
              685.0
            ],
            [
              84.568359375,
              685.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/129/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>If the global value is mutable, you can modify it without declaring it:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              688.74609375
            ],
            [
              390.26953125,
              688.74609375
            ],
            [
              390.26953125,
              701.12109375
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              701.12109375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        }
      ],
      "section_hierarchy": {
        "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
        "3": "/page/129/SectionHeader/1"
      },
      "images": null
    },
    {
      "id": "/page/130/Page/178",
      "block_type": "Page",
