        },
        {
          "id": "/page/132/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 11.11.</b> <i>Here's another Puzzler from</i> Car Talk (http: // www. cartalk. com/ content/ puzzlers ):</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              333.0
            ],
            [
              525.638671875,
              333.0
            ],
            [
              525.638671875,
              355.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              355.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/132/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/132/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>This was sent in by a fellow named Dan O'Leary. He came upon a common one-syllable,</i> <i>five-letter word recently that has the following unique property. When you remove the</i> <i>first letter, the remaining letters form a homophone of the original word, that is a word</i> <i>that sounds exactly the same. Replace the first letter, that is, put it back and remove</i> <i>the second letter and the result is yet another homophone of the original word. And the</i> <i>question is, what's the word?</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              154.0458984375,
              368.15625
            ],
            [
              501.0,
              368.15625
            ],
            [
              501.0,
              440.0
            ],
            [
              154.0458984375,
              440.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/132/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/132/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>Now I'm going to give you an example that doesn't work. Let's look at the five-letter</i> <i>word, 'wrack.' W-R-A-C-K, you know like to 'wrack with pain.' If I remove the first</i> <i>letter, I am left with a four-letter word, 'R-A-C-K.' As in, 'Holy cow, did you see the</i> <i>rack on that buck! It must have been a nine-pointer!' It's a perfect homophone. If you</i> <i>put the 'w' back, and remove the 'r,' instead, you're left with the word, 'wack,' which is</i> <i>a real word, it's just not a homophone of the other two words.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              153.896484375,
              446.0
            ],
            [
              501.0,
              446.0
            ],
            [
              501.0,
              517.81640625
            ],
            [
              153.896484375,
              517.81640625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/132/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/132/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>But there is, however, at least one word that Dan and we know of, which will yield two</i> <i>homophones if you remove either of the first two letters to make two, new four-letter</i> <i>words. The question is, what's the word?</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              153.7470703125,
              523.0
            ],
            [
              501.43359375,
              523.0
            ],
            [
              501.43359375,
              558.03515625
            ],
            [
              153.7470703125,
              558.03515625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/132/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/132/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>You can use the dictionary from Exercise 11.1 to check whether a string is in the word list.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.09375,
              571.95703125
            ],
            [
              490.0,
              571.95703125
            ],
            [
              490.0,
              582.0
            ],
            [
              129.09375,
              582.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/122/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/132/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/132/Text/14",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>To check whether two words are homophones, you can use the CMU Pronouncing Dictionary.</i> <i>You can download it from</i> http: // www. speech. cs. cmu. edu/ cgi-bin/ cmudict <i>or from</i> http: // thinkpython. com/ code/ c06d <i>and you can also download</i> http: // thinkpython. com/ code/ pronounce. py <i>, which provides a function named</i> read_dictionary <i>that reads the</i> <i>pronouncing dictionary and returns a Python dictionary that maps from each word to a string that</i> <i>describes its primary pronunciation.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.392578125,
              592.0
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              592.0
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              663.0
