          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/SectionHeader/1",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>12.3 Tuples as return values</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              86.0
            ],
            [
              320.0,
              85.02978515625
            ],
            [
              320.0,
              100.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              101.513671875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Strictly speaking, a function can only return one value, but if the value is a tuple, the effect is the same as returning multiple values. For example, if you want to divide two integers and compute the quotient and remainder, it is inefficient to compute x/y and then x%y. It is better to compute them both at the same time.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              111.4716796875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              111.4716796875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              159.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              159.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The built-in function divmod takes two arguments and returns a tuple of two values, the quotient and remainder. You can store the result as a tuple:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              167.255859375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              167.255859375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              190.072265625
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              190.072265625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Code/4",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; t = divmod(7, 3)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print t\n(2, 1)\nOr use tuple assignment to store the elements separately:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; quot, rem = divmod(7, 3)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print quot\n2\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print rem\n1</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              130.0,
              194.90625
            ],
            [
              381.0,
              194.90625
            ],
            [
              381.0,
              310.0
            ],
            [
              129.16845703125,
              310.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Here is an example of a function that returns a tuple:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              127.7490234375,
              315.755859375
            ],
            [
              362.1796875,
              315.755859375
            ],
            [
              362.1796875,
              326.0
            ],
            [
              127.7490234375,
              326.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>def min_max(t):</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              130.0,
              330.451171875
            ],
            [
              208.0,
              330.451171875
            ],
            [
              208.0,
              341.0
            ],
            [
              130.0,
              341.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Code/7",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>return min(t), max(t)</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              145.23046875,
              341.47265625
            ],
            [
              260.0,
              341.47265625
            ],
            [
              260.0,
              353.0
            ],
            [
              145.23046875,
              353.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>max and min are built-in functions that find the largest and smallest elements of a sequence. min_max computes both and returns a tuple of two values.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              358.1015625
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              358.1015625
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              381.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              381.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/SectionHeader/9",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>12.4 Variable-length argument tuples</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.197265625,
              408.375
            ],
            [
              382.201171875,
              408.375
            ],
            [
              382.201171875,
              424.0
            ],
            [
              128.197265625,
              424.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Functions can take a variable number of arguments. A parameter name that begins with * <b>gathers</b> arguments into a tuple. For example, printall takes any number of arguments and prints them:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.392578125,
              435.4453125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              435.4453125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              471.0234375
            ],
            [
              129.392578125,
              471.0234375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Code/11",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>def printall(*args):</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              130.0,
              475.0
            ],
            [
              234.0,
              475.0
            ],
            [
              234.0,
              486.87890625
            ],
            [
              130.0,
              486.87890625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>print args</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              145.45458984375,
              488.0
            ],
            [
              203.0,
              488.0
            ],
            [
              203.0,
              499.25390625
            ],
            [
              145.45458984375,
              499.25390625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The gather parameter can have any name you like, but args is conventional. Here's how the function works: ''</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              503.0
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              503.0
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              528.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              528.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Code/14",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; printall(1, 2.0, 3)\n(1, 2.0, '3')</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.8408203125,
              530.0
            ],
            [
              260.0,
              530.0
            ],
            [
              260.0,
              553.0
            ],
            [
              129.8408203125,
              553.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Text/15",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The complement of gather is <b>scatter</b>. If you have a sequence of values and you want to pass it to a function as multiple arguments, you can use the * operator. For example, divmod takes exactly two arguments; it doesn't work with a tuple:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              558.421875
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              558.421875
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              593.2265625
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              593.2265625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Code/16",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; t = (7, 3)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; divmod(t)\nTypeError: divmod expected 2 arguments, got 1</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              599.0
            ],
            [
              365.466796875,
              599.0
            ],
            [
              365.466796875,
              639.6328125
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              639.6328125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Text/17",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>But if you scatter the tuple, it works:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.8408203125,
              639.0
            ],
            [
              290.0,
              639.0
            ],
            [
              290.0,
              654.71484375
            ],
            [
              129.8408203125,
              654.71484375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/TextInlineMath/18",
          "block_type": "TextInlineMath",
          "html": "<p>&gt;&gt;&gt; divmod(*t) (2, 1)</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              654.0
            ],
            [
              203.0,
              654.0
            ],
            [
              203.0,
              676.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              676.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/136/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/136/Text/19",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 12.1.</b> <i>Many of the built-in functions use variable-length argument tuples. For example,</i> max and min <i>can take any number of arguments:</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              676.7578125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              676.7578125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              701.0
