        {
          "id": "/page/137/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>If you combine zip, for and tuple assignment, you get a useful idiom for traversing two (or more) sequences at the same time. For example, has_match takes two sequences, t1 and t2, and returns True if there is an index i such that t1[i] == t2[i]:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              543.7265625
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              543.7265625
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              579.3046875
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              579.3046875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/137/SectionHeader/2"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/137/Code/13",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>def has_match(t1, t2):\n    for x, y in zip(t1, t2):\n        if x == y:\n            return True\n    return False</pre>",
          "polygon": [
