          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/141/ListGroup/208",
          "block_type": "ListGroup",
          "html": "<p><ul><content-ref src='/page/141/ListItem/1'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/141/ListItem/2'></content-ref></ul></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              98.015625,
              87.8818359375
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              87.8818359375
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              141.0
            ],
            [
              98.015625,
              141.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "id": "/page/141/ListItem/1",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li>2. If you want to use a sequence as a dictionary key, you have to use an immutable type like a tuple or string.</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  98.015625,
                  87.8818359375
                ],
                [
                  482.90625,
                  87.8818359375
                ],
                [
                  482.90625,
                  111.0
                ],
                [
                  98.015625,
                  111.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/140/SectionHeader/10"
              },
              "images": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "/page/141/ListItem/2",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li>3. If you are passing a sequence as an argument to a function, using tuples reduces the potential for unexpected behavior due to aliasing.</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  98.015625,
                  118.4326171875
                ],
                [
                  482.30859375,
                  118.4326171875
                ],
                [
                  482.30859375,
                  141.0
                ],
                [
                  98.015625,
                  141.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/140/SectionHeader/10"
              },
              "images": {}
            }
          ],
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/140/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": null
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/141/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Because tuples are immutable, they don't provide methods like sort and reverse, which modify existing lists. But Python provides the built-in functions sorted and reversed, which take any sequence as a parameter and return a new list with the same elements in a different order.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              148.9833984375
            ],
            [
              482.607421875,
              148.9833984375
            ],
            [
              482.607421875,
              196.1630859375
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              196.1630859375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/141/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/141/SectionHeader/4",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>12.9 Debugging</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              223.0
            ],
            [
              200.9619140625,
              221.9765625
            ],
            [
              200.9619140625,
              237.05859375
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              238.60546875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/141/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/141/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Lists, dictionaries and tuples are known generically as <b>data structures</b>; in this chapter we are starting to see compound data structures, like lists of tuples, and dictionaries that contain tuples as keys and lists as values. Compound data structures are useful, but they are prone to what I call <b>shape errors</b>; that is, errors caused when a data structure has the wrong type, size or composition. For example, if you are expecting a list with one integer and I give you a plain old integer (not in a list), it won't work.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              246.33984375
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              246.33984375
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              318.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              318.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/141/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/141/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>To help debug these kinds of errors, I have written a module called structshape that provides a function, also called structshape, that takes any kind of data structure as an argument and returns a string that summarizes its shape. You can download it from http://thinkpython.com/code/structshape.py</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              323.68359375
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              323.68359375
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              372.0234375
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              372.0234375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/141/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/141/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Here's the result for a simple list:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              84.8671875,
              379.37109375
            ],
            [
              234.28125,
              379.37109375
            ],
            [
              234.28125,
              390.0
            ],
            [
              84.8671875,
              390.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/141/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/141/Code/8",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; from structshape import structshape\n&gt;&gt;&gt; t = [1,2,3]\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print structshape(t)\nlist of 3 int\nA fancier program might write \"list of 3 ints,\" but it was easier not to deal with plurals.\nHere's a list of lists:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; t2 = [[1,2], [3,4], [5,6]]\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print structshape(t2)\nlist of 3 list of 2 int\nIf the elements of the list are not the same type, structshape groups them, in order, by\ntype:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; t3 = [1, 2, 3, 4.0, '5', '6', [7], [8], 9]\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print structshape(t3)\nlist of (3 int, float, 2 str, 2 list of int, int)\nHere's a list of tuples:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; s = 'abc'\n              \n&gt;&gt;&gt; lt = zip(t, s)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print structshape(lt)\nlist of 3 tuple of (int, str)\nAnd here's a dictionary with 3 items that map integers to strings.\n&gt;&gt;&gt; d = dict(lt)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print structshape(d)\ndict of 3 int-&gt;str\nIf you are having trouble keeping track of your data structures, structshape can help.</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.0166015625,
              387.4921875
            ],
            [
              485.89453125,
              387.4921875
            ],
            [
              485.89453125,
              701.0
            ],
            [
              85.0166015625,
              701.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/134/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/141/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        }
      ],
