          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/150/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>Credit: This case study is based on an example from Kernighan and Pike,</i> The Practice of Programming<i>, Addison-Wesley, 1999.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.5419921875,
              521.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              521.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              544.0
            ],
            [
              129.5419921875,
              544.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/144/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/149/SectionHeader/11"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/150/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>You should attempt this exercise before you go on; then you can can download my solution from http://thinkpython.com/code/markov.py. You will also need http:// thinkpython.com/code/emma.txt.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              554.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              554.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              588.0
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              588.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/144/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/150/SectionHeader/14"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/150/SectionHeader/14",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>13.9 Data structures</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.09375,
              616.81640625
            ],
            [
              269.244140625,
              616.81640625
            ],
            [
              269.244140625,
              631.0
            ],
            [
              129.09375,
              631.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/144/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/150/SectionHeader/14"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/150/Text/15",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Using Markov analysis to generate random text is fun, but there is also a point to this exercise: data structure selection. In your solution to the previous exercises, you had to choose:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              643.0
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              643.0
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              678.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              678.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/144/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/150/SectionHeader/14"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/150/ListItem/16",
          "block_type": "ListItem",
          "html": "<li> How to represent the prefixes.</li>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              143.0,
              689.90625
            ],
