            "3": "/page/154/SectionHeader/2"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/154/SectionHeader/2",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>14.1 Persistence</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              291.392578125
            ],
            [
              242.0,
              291.392578125
            ],
            [
              242.0,
              306.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              306.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/154/SectionHeader/2"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/154/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Most of the programs we have seen so far are transient in the sense that they run for a short time and produce some output, but when they end, their data disappears. If you run the program again, it starts with a clean slate.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              317.689453125
            ],
            [
              527.73046875,
              317.689453125
            ],
            [
              527.73046875,
              352.494140625
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              352.494140625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/154/SectionHeader/2"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/154/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Other programs are <b>persistent</b>: they run for a long time (or all the time); they keep at least some of their data in permanent storage (a hard drive, for example); and if they shut down and restart, they pick up where they left off.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              361.58203125
            ],
            [
              527.73046875,
              361.58203125
            ],
            [
              527.73046875,
              396.7734375
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              396.7734375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/154/SectionHeader/2"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/154/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Examples of persistent programs are operating systems, which run pretty much whenever a computer is on, and web servers, which run all the time, waiting for requests to come in on the network.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              404.89453125
            ],
            [
              528.328125,
              404.89453125
            ],
            [
              528.328125,
              440.47265625
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              440.47265625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/154/SectionHeader/2"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/154/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>One of the simplest ways for programs to maintain their data is by reading and writing text files. We have already seen programs that read text files; in this chapter we will see programs that write them.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              449.3671875
            ],
            [
              528.626953125,
              449.3671875
            ],
            [
              528.626953125,
              485.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              485.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/154/SectionHeader/2"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/154/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>An alternative is to store the state of the program in a database. In this chapter I will present a simple database and a module, pickle, that makes it easy to store program data.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              494.0
            ],
            [
              528.328125,
              494.0
            ],
            [
              528.328125,
              517.4296875
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              517.4296875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/154/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/154/SectionHeader/8",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>14.2 Reading and writing</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.86962890625,
              544.5
            ],
            [
              306.0,
              544.5
            ],
            [
              306.0,
              559.1953125
            ],
            [
              128.86962890625,
              559.1953125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/154/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/154/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>A text file is a sequence of characters stored on a permanent medium like a hard drive, flash memory, or CD-ROM. We saw how to open and read a file in Section 9.1. ''</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.197265625,
              570.41015625
            ],
            [
              527.431640625,
              570.41015625
            ],
            [
              527.431640625,
              600.0
            ],
            [
              128.197265625,
              600.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/154/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/154/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>To write a file, you have to open it with mode w as a second parameter: ''''</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              127.8984375,
              602.12109375
            ],
            [
              451.828125,
              602.12109375
            ],
            [
              451.828125,
              615.0
            ],
            [
              127.8984375,
              615.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/154/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/154/Code/11",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; fout = open(output.txt, w)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print fout\n&lt;open file 'output.txt', mode 'w' at 0xb7eb2410&gt;</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.31787109375,
              619.0
            ],
            [
              382.201171875,
              619.0
            ],
            [
              382.201171875,
              661.2890625
            ],
            [
              129.31787109375,
              661.2890625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/154/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/154/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>If the file already exists, opening it in write mode clears out the old data and starts fresh, so be careful! If the file doesn't exist, a new one is created.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              127.8984375,
              659.0
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              659.0
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              681.78515625
            ],
            [
              127.8984375,
              681.78515625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/154/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/154/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The write method puts data into the file.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.72021484375,
              690.29296875
            ],
            [
              310.482421875,
              690.29296875
            ],
            [
              310.482421875,
              701.0
