          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/156/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The format operator is powerful, but it can be difficult to use. You can read more about it at http://docs.python.org/2/library/stdtypes.html#string-formatting.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.197265625,
              120.3662109375
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              120.3662109375
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              143.0
            ],
            [
              128.197265625,
              143.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/156/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/156/SectionHeader/3",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>14.4 Filenames and paths</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              172.283203125
            ],
            [
              304.0,
              172.283203125
            ],
            [
              304.0,
              187.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              187.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/156/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/156/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Files are organized into <b>directories</b> (also called \"folders\"). Every running program has a \"current directory,\" which is the default directory for most operations. For example, when you open a file for reading, Python looks for it in the current directory.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              198.966796875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              198.966796875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              234.0
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              234.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/156/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/156/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The os module provides functions for working with files and directories (\"os\" stands for \"operating system\"). os.getcwd returns the name of the current directory:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              243.826171875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              243.826171875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              266.0
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              266.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/156/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/156/Code/6",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; import os\n&gt;&gt;&gt; cwd = os.getcwd()\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print cwd\n/home/dinsdale</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              273.0
            ],
            [
              239.8095703125,
              273.0
            ],
            [
              239.8095703125,
              321.943359375
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              321.943359375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/156/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/156/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>cwd stands for \"current working directory.\" The result in this example is /home/dinsdale, which is the home directory of a user named dinsdale.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.09375,
              325.0
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              325.0
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              348.0
            ],
            [
              129.09375,
              348.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/156/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/156/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>A string like cwd that identifies a file is called a <b>path</b>. A <b>relative path</b> starts from the current directory; an <b>absolute path</b> starts from the topmost directory in the file system.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              357.134765625
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              357.134765625
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              380.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              380.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/156/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/156/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The paths we have seen so far are simple filenames, so they are relative to the current directory. To find the absolute path to a file, you can use os.path.abspath: ''</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              389.42578125
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              389.42578125
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              415.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              415.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/156/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/156/Code/10",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; os.path.abspath(memo.txt)\n'/home/dinsdale/memo.txt'\n                            \nos.path.exists checks whether a file or directory exists:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; os.path.exists('memo.txt')\nTrue\nIf it exists, os.path.isdir checks whether it's a directory:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; os.path.isdir('memo.txt')\nFalse\n&gt;&gt;&gt; os.path.isdir('music')\nTrue\nSimilarly, os.path.isfile checks whether it's a file.\nos.listdir returns a list of the files (and other directories) in the given directory:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; os.listdir(cwd)\n['music', 'photos', 'memo.txt']\nTo demonstrate these functions, the following example \"walks\" through a directory, prints\nthe names of all the files, and calls itself recursively on all the directories.\ndef walk(dirname):\n    for name in os.listdir(dirname):\n         path = os.path.join(dirname, name)</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.2431640625,
              417.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              417.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              688.0
            ],
            [
              129.2431640625,
              688.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/154/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/156/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        }
      ],
      "section_hierarchy": {
