            "3": "/page/169/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/169/SectionHeader/9",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>15.7 Debugging</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              493.06640625
            ],
            [
              200.0,
              493.06640625
            ],
            [
              200.0,
              508.53515625
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              508.53515625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/164/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/169/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/169/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>When you start working with objects, you are likely to encounter some new exceptions. If you try to access an attribute that doesn't exist, you get an AttributeError:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              518.58984375
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              518.58984375
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              541.01953125
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              541.01953125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/164/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/169/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/169/Code/11",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; p = Point()\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print p.z\nAttributeError: Point instance has no attribute 'z'\n                                                          \nIf you are not sure what type an object is, you can ask:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; type(p)\n&lt;type '__main__.Point'&gt;\nIf you are not sure whether an object has a particular attribute, you can use the built-in\nfunction hasattr:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; hasattr(p, 'x')\nTrue\n&gt;&gt;&gt; hasattr(p, 'z')\nFalse</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              543.7265625
            ],
            [
              482.0,
              543.7265625
            ],
            [
              482.0,
              701.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              701.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/164/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/169/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        }
