        {
          "id": "/page/180/Text/1",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>By convention, the first parameter of a method is called self, so it would be more common to write print_time like this:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              88.80029296875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              88.80029296875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              111.0
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              111.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/179/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/180/Code/2",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>class Time(object):\n    def print_time(self):\n        print '%.2d:%.2d:%.2d' % (self.hour, self.minute, self.second)</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.69140625,
              117.0
            ],
            [
              496.0,
              117.0
            ],
            [
              496.0,
              152.947265625
            ],
            [
              129.69140625,
              152.947265625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/179/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/180/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The reason for this convention is an implicit metaphor:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              158.0
            ],
            [
              371.0,
              158.0
            ],
            [
              371.0,
              168.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              168.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/179/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/180/ListGroup/204",
          "block_type": "ListGroup",
          "html": "<p><ul><content-ref src='/page/180/ListItem/4'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/180/ListItem/5'></content-ref></ul></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              142.83984375,
              181.7578125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              181.7578125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              250.0
            ],
            [
              142.83984375,
              250.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "id": "/page/180/ListItem/4",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li> The syntax for a function call, print_time(start), suggests that the function is the active agent. It says something like, \"Hey print_time! Here's an object for you to print.\"</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  142.83984375,
                  181.7578125
                ],
                [
                  526.0,
                  181.7578125
                ],
                [
                  526.0,
                  217.0
                ],
                [
                  142.83984375,
                  217.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/179/SectionHeader/4"
              },
              "images": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "/page/180/ListItem/5",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li> In object-oriented programming, the objects are the active agents. A method invocation like start.print_time() says \"Hey start! Please print yourself.\"</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  143.0,
                  227.390625
                ],
                [
                  526.0,
                  227.390625
                ],
                [
                  526.0,
                  250.0
                ],
                [
                  143.0,
                  250.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/179/SectionHeader/4"
              },
              "images": {}
            }
          ],
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/179/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": null
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/180/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>This change in perspective might be more polite, but it is not obvious that it is useful. In the examples we have seen so far, it may not be. But sometimes shifting responsibility from the functions onto the objects makes it possible to write more versatile functions, and makes it easier to maintain and reuse code.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.09375,
              264.12890625
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              264.12890625
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              311.0
            ],
            [
              129.09375,
              311.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/179/SectionHeader/4"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/180/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 17.1.</b> <i>Rewrite</i> time_to_int <i>(from Section 16.4) as a method. It is probably not appro</i><i>priate to rewrite</i> int_to_time <i>as a method; what object you would invoke it on?</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
