          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/SectionHeader/1",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>17.6 The</b> __str__ <b>method</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              127.7490234375,
              84.0
            ],
            [
              303.0,
              84.0
            ],
            [
              303.0,
              100.0
            ],
            [
              127.7490234375,
              101.0302734375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>__str__ is a special method, like __init__, that is supposed to return a string representation of an object.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              115.9189453125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              115.9189453125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              139.1220703125
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              139.1220703125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>For example, here is a str method for Time objects:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              151.400390625
            ],
            [
              355.0,
              151.400390625
            ],
            [
              355.0,
              162.0
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              162.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Code/4",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre># inside class Time:</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              170.2529296875
            ],
            [
              234.0,
              170.2529296875
            ],
            [
              234.0,
              181.0
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              181.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Code/5",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>def __str__(self):\n   return '%.2d:%.2d:%.2d' % (self.hour, self.minute, self.second)</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              147.1728515625,
              195.0
            ],
            [
              501.0,
              195.0
            ],
            [
              501.0,
              218.8828125
            ],
            [
              147.1728515625,
              218.8828125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>When you print an object, Python invokes the str method:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.16845703125,
              227.0
            ],
            [
              393.85546875,
              227.0
            ],
            [
              393.85546875,
              237.0
            ],
            [
              129.16845703125,
              237.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Code/7",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; time = Time(9, 45)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print time\n09:45:00</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.27197265625,
              244.986328125
            ],
            [
              245.0,
              244.986328125
            ],
            [
              245.0,
              281.0
            ],
            [
              128.27197265625,
              281.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>When I write a new class, I almost always start by writing __init__, which makes it easier to instantiate objects, and __str__, which is useful for debugging.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.09375,
              290.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              290.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              312.85546875
            ],
            [
              129.09375,
              312.85546875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 17.3.</b> <i>Write a</i> str <i>method for the</i> Point <i>class. Create a Point object and print it.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.5419921875,
              313.62890625
            ],
            [
              492.46875,
              313.62890625
            ],
            [
              492.46875,
              324.0
            ],
            [
              129.5419921875,
              324.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/SectionHeader/10",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>17.7 Operator overloading</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.197265625,
              361.58203125
            ],
            [
              311.9765625,
              361.58203125
            ],
            [
              311.9765625,
              377.0
            ],
            [
              128.197265625,
              377.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>By defining other special methods, you can specify the behavior of operators on userdefined types. For example, if you define a method named __add__ for the Time class, you can use the + operator on Time objects.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              393.099609375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              393.099609375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              428.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              428.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Here is what the definition might look like:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              440.47265625
            ],
            [
              319.1484375,
              440.47265625
            ],
            [
              319.1484375,
              452.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              452.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Code/13",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre># inside class Time:\n    def __add__(self, other):\n        seconds = self.time_to_int() + other.time_to_int()\n        return int_to_time(seconds)</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              130.0,
              461.0
            ],
            [
              433.0,
              461.0
            ],
            [
              433.0,
              519.0
            ],
            [
              130.0,
              519.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Text/14",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>And here is how you could use it:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.5419921875,
              528.64453125
            ],
            [
              278.0,
              528.64453125
            ],
            [
              278.0,
              539.0
            ],
            [
              129.5419921875,
              539.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Code/15",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; start = Time(9, 45)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; duration = Time(1, 35)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print start + duration\n11:20:00</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.69140625,
              548.0
            ],
            [
              266.0,
              548.0
            ],
            [
              266.0,
              595.0
            ],
            [
              129.69140625,
              595.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Text/16",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>When you apply the + operator to Time objects, Python invokes __add__. When you print the result, Python invokes __str__. So there is quite a lot happening behind the scenes!</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              604.0
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              604.0
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              626.87109375
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              626.87109375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Text/17",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Changing the behavior of an operator so that it works with user-defined types is called op<b>erator overloading</b>. For every operator in Python there is a corresponding special method, like __add__. For more details, see http://docs.python.org/2/reference/datamodel. html#specialnames.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              638.859375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              638.859375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              686.8125
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              686.8125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/182/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/182/Text/18",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 17.4.</b> <i>Write an</i> add <i>method for the Point class.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
