        {
          "id": "/page/185/SectionHeader/1",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>17.10 Debugging</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              84.884765625
            ],
            [
              207.38671875,
              83.337890625
            ],
            [
              207.38671875,
              100.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              101.3203125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>It is legal to add attributes to objects at any point in the execution of a program, but if you are a stickler for type theory, it is a dubious practice to have objects of the same type with different attribute sets. It is usually a good idea to initialize all of an object's attributes in the init method.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              110.6015625
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              109.0546875
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              158.0
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              158.361328125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>If you are not sure whether an object has a particular attribute, you can use the built-in function hasattr (see Section 15.7).</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              165.9990234375
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              164.4521484375
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              190.0
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              190.1689453125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Another way to access the attributes of an object is through the special attribute __dict__, which is a dictionary that maps attribute names (as strings) and values:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              198.966796875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              197.419921875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              221.0
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              222.169921875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/TextInlineMath/5",
          "block_type": "TextInlineMath",
          "html": "<p>&gt;&gt;&gt; p = Point(3, 4) &gt;&gt;&gt; print p.__dict__ {'y': 4, 'x': 3}</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              83.8212890625,
              223.91015625
            ],
            [
              269.0947265625,
              223.91015625
            ],
            [
              269.0947265625,
              273.796875
            ],
            [
              83.8212890625,
              273.796875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>For purposes of debugging, you might find it useful to keep this function handy: def print_attributes(obj):</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              266.0
            ],
            [
              441.66796875,
              266.0
            ],
            [
              441.66796875,
              296.419921875
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              296.419921875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>for attr in obj.__dict__: print attr, getattr(obj, attr)</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              107.0,
              294.0
            ],
            [
              285.0,
              294.0
            ],
            [
              285.0,
              316.0
            ],
            [
              107.0,
              316.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>print_attributes traverses the items in the object's dictionary and prints each attribute name and its corresponding value.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              84.8671875,
              318.26953125
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              318.26953125
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              343.01953125
            ],
            [
              84.8671875,
              343.01953125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The built-in function getattr takes an object and an attribute name (as a string) and returns the attribute's value.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.3154296875,
              350.3671875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              350.3671875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              375.0
            ],
            [
              85.3154296875,
              375.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/SectionHeader/10",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>17.11 Interface and implementation</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              399.09375
            ],
            [
              328.412109375,
              399.09375
            ],
            [
              328.412109375,
              417.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              417.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>One of the goals of object-oriented design is to make software more maintainable, which means that you can keep the program working when other parts of the system change, and modify the program to meet new requirements.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.3154296875,
              426.1640625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              426.1640625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              463.2890625
            ],
            [
              85.3154296875,
              463.2890625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>A design principle that helps achieve that goal is to keep interfaces separate from implementations. For objects, that means that the methods a class provides should not depend on how the attributes are represented.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              471.0234375
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              471.0234375
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              507.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              507.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>For example, in this chapter we developed a class that represents a time of day. Methods provided by this class include time_to_int, is_after, and add_time.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              514.72265625
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              514.72265625
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              538.69921875
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              538.69921875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/Text/14",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>We could implement those methods in several ways. The details of the implementation depend on how we represent time. In this chapter, the attributes of a Time object are hour, minute, and second.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.3154296875,
              546.046875
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              546.046875
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              582.0
            ],
            [
              85.3154296875,
              582.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/Text/15",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>As an alternative, we could replace these attributes with a single integer representing the number of seconds since midnight. This implementation would make some methods, like is_after, easier to write, but it makes some methods harder.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              590.1328125
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              590.1328125
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              626.0
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              626.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/Text/16",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>After you deploy a new class, you might discover a better implementation. If other parts of the program are using your class, it might be time-consuming and error-prone to change the interface.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              632.671875
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              632.671875
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              669.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              669.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/185/Text/17",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p class=\"has-continuation\">But if you designed the interface carefully, you can change the implementation without changing the interface, which means that other parts of the program don't have to change.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              677.53125
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              677.53125
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              701.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              701.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        }
      ],
      "section_hierarchy": {
        "1": "/page/178/SectionHeader/1",
        "3": "/page/185/SectionHeader/10"
      },
