          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/186/Text/1",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Keeping the interface separate from the implementation means that you have to hide the attributes. Code in other parts of the program (outside the class definition) should use methods to read and modify the state of the object. They should not access the attributes directly. This principle is called <b>information hiding</b>; see http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ Information_hiding.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              88.70361328125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              88.70361328125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              148.0
            ],
            [
