        {
          "id": "/page/190/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Putting it all together, the expression Card.rank_names[self.rank] means \"use the attribute rank from the object self as an index into the list rank_names from the class Card, and select the appropriate string.\"</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.2431640625,
              257.16796875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              257.16796875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              292.0
            ],
            [
              129.2431640625,
              292.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/189/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/190/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The first element of rank_names is None because there is no card with rank zero. By including None as a place-keeper, we get a mapping with the nice property that the index 2 maps to the string '2', and so on. To avoid this tweak, we could have used a dictionary instead of a list.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              303.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              303.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              350.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              350.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/189/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/190/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>With the methods we have so far, we can create and print cards:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.57080078125,
              359.841796875
            ],
            [
              410.0,
              359.841796875
            ],
            [
              410.0,
              370.0
            ],
            [
              128.57080078125,
              370.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/189/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/190/Code/6",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; card1 = Card(2, 11)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print card1\nJack of Hearts</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              375.697265625
            ],
            [
              250.0,
              375.697265625
            ],
            [
              250.0,
              411.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              411.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/189/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/190/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Figure 18.1 is a diagram of the Card class object and one Card instance. Card is a class object, so it has type type. card1 has type Card. (To save space, I didn't draw the contents of suit_names and rank_names).</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              418.0
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              418.0
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              452.07421875
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              452.07421875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/190/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/190/SectionHeader/8",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>18.3 Comparing cards</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              481.46484375
            ],
            [
              281.0,
              481.46484375
            ],
            [
              281.0,
              498.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              498.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/190/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/190/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>For built-in types, there are relational operators (&lt;, &gt;, ==, etc.) that compare values and determine when one is greater than, less than, or equal to another. For user-defined types, we can override the behavior of the built-in operators by providing a method named __cmp__.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              509.30859375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              509.30859375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              545.0
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              545.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/190/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/190/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>__cmp__ takes two parameters, self and other, and returns a positive number if the first object is greater, a negative number if the second object is greater, and 0 if they are equal to each other.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              554.5546875
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              554.5546875
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              590.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              590.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/190/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/190/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The correct ordering for cards is not obvious. For example, which is better, the 3 of Clubs or the 2 of Diamonds? One has a higher rank, but the other has a higher suit. In order to compare cards, you have to decide whether rank or suit is more important.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              599.4140625
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              599.4140625
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              635.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              635.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/190/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/190/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The answer might depend on what game you are playing, but to keep things simple, we'll make the arbitrary choice that suit is more important, so all of the Spades outrank all of the Diamonds, and so on.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              645.43359375
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              645.43359375
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              680.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              680.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/190/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/190/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>With that decided, we can write __cmp__:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.31787109375,
              691.0
            ],
            [
              311.0,
              691.0
            ],
            [
              311.0,
              701.12109375
            ],
            [
              129.31787109375,
              701.12109375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/190/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        }
      ],
      "section_hierarchy": {
        "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
        "3": "/page/190/SectionHeader/8"
