          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/193/Text/1",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>In this case we are defining a \"thin\" method that expresses a list operation in terms that are appropriate for decks.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.3154296875,
              87.6884765625
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              87.6884765625
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              111.0
            ],
            [
              85.3154296875,
              111.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/192/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/193/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>As another example, we can write a Deck method named shuffle using the function shuffle from the random module:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              120.3662109375
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              120.3662109375
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              143.0
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              143.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/192/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/193/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p># inside class Deck:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              149.1767578125
            ],
            [
              191.0,
              149.1767578125
            ],
            [
              191.0,
              160.0
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              160.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/192/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/193/Code/4",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>def shuffle(self):\n    random.shuffle(self.cards)</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              105.1875,
              174.0
            ],
            [
              265.060546875,
              174.0
            ],
            [
              265.060546875,
              198.0
            ],
            [
              105.1875,
              198.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/192/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/193/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Don't forget to import random.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              202.0
            ],
            [
              220.236328125,
              202.0
            ],
            [
              220.236328125,
              212.501953125
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              212.501953125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/192/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/193/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 18.2.</b> <i>Write a Deck method named</i> sort <i>that uses the list method</i> sort <i>to sort the cards</i> <i>in a</i> Deck. sort <i>uses the</i> __cmp__ <i>method we defined to determine sort order.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              214.435546875
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              214.435546875
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              237.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              237.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>18.7 Inheritance</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.3154296875,
              266.0
            ],
            [
              201.0,
              266.0
            ],
            [
              201.0,
              281.0
            ],
            [
              85.3154296875,
              281.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/193/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The language feature most often associated with object-oriented programming is <b>inher</b><b>itance</b>. Inheritance is the ability to define a new class that is a modified version of an existing class.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              292.939453125
            ],
            [
              482.0,
              292.939453125
            ],
            [
              482.0,
              328.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              328.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/193/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>It is called \"inheritance\" because the new class inherits the methods of the existing class. Extending this metaphor, the existing class is called the <b>parent</b> and the new class is called the <b>child</b>.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              336.83203125
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              336.83203125
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              372.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              372.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/193/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>As an example, let's say we want a class to represent a \"hand,\" that is, the set of cards held by one player. A hand is similar to a deck: both are made up of a set of cards, and both require operations like adding and removing cards.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              381.69140625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              381.69140625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              417.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              417.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/193/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>A hand is also different from a deck; there are operations we want for hands that don't make sense for a deck. For example, in poker we might compare two hands to see which one wins. In bridge, we might compute a score for a hand in order to make a bid.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              425.77734375
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              425.77734375
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              461.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              461.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/193/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>This relationship between classes—similar, but different—lends itself to inheritance.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              471.0234375
            ],
            [
              455.4140625,
              471.0234375
            ],
            [
              455.4140625,
              482.0
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              482.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/193/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The definition of a child class is like other class definitions, but the name of the parent class appears in parentheses:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              490.74609375
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              490.74609375
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              514.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              514.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
