        {
          "id": "/page/194/Text/1",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>So when you create a Hand, Python invokes this init method: ''</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              127.8984375,
              88.9453125
            ],
            [
              399.0,
              88.9453125
            ],
            [
              399.0,
              101.0
            ],
            [
              127.8984375,
              101.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/194/Code/2",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>&gt;&gt;&gt; hand = Hand(new hand)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print hand.cards\n[]\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print hand.label\nnew hand\nBut the other methods are inherited from Deck, so we can use pop_card and add_card to\ndeal a card:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; deck = Deck()\n&gt;&gt;&gt; card = deck.pop_card()\n&gt;&gt;&gt; hand.add_card(card)\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print hand\nKing of Spades\nA natural next step is to encapsulate this code in a method called move_cards:\n#inside class Deck:</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              104.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              104.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              286.0
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              286.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/194/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>def move_cards(self, hand, num):</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              148.44287109375,
              300.673828125
            ],
            [
              318.849609375,
              300.673828125
            ],
            [
              318.849609375,
              311.0
            ],
            [
              148.44287109375,
              311.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/194/Code/4",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>for i in range(num):\n    hand.add_card(self.pop_card())</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              170.033203125,
              311.888671875
            ],
            [
              349.03125,
              311.888671875
            ],
            [
              349.03125,
              335.0
            ],
            [
              170.033203125,
              335.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/194/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>move_cards takes two arguments, a Hand object and the number of cards to deal. It modifies both self and hand, and returns None.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              339.92578125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              339.92578125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              363.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              363.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/194/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>In some games, cards are moved from one hand to another, or from a hand back to the deck. You can use move_cards for any of these operations: self can be either a Deck or a Hand, and hand, despite the name, can also be a Deck.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              372.0234375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              372.0234375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              407.6015625
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              407.6015625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/194/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 18.3.</b> <i>Write a Deck method called</i> deal_hands <i>that takes two parameters, the number of</i> <i>hands and the number of cards per hand, and that creates new Hand objects, deals the appropriate</i> <i>number of cards per hand, and returns a list of Hand objects.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              409.0
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              409.0
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              443.1796875
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              443.1796875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/194/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Inheritance is a useful feature. Some programs that would be repetitive without inheritance can be written more elegantly with it. Inheritance can facilitate code reuse, since you can customize the behavior of parent classes without having to modify them. In some cases, the inheritance structure reflects the natural structure of the problem, which makes the program easier to understand.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              452.84765625
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              452.84765625
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              512.0
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              512.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/193/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/194/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>On the other hand, inheritance can make programs difficult to read. When a method is invoked, it is sometimes not clear where to find its definition. The relevant code may be scattered among several modules. Also, many of the things that can be done using inheritance can be done as well or better without it.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              520.91015625
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              520.91015625
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              568.0
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              568.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/194/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/194/SectionHeader/10",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>18.8 Class diagrams</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.72021484375,
              596.0
            ],
            [
              268.0,
              596.0
            ],
            [
              268.0,
              611.0
