          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/194/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/194/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>So far we have seen stack diagrams, which show the state of a program, and object diagrams, which show the attributes of an object and their values. These diagrams represent a snapshot in the execution of a program, so they change as the program runs.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.197265625,
              622.23046875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              622.23046875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              657.0
            ],
            [
              128.197265625,
              657.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/194/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/194/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>They are also highly detailed; for some purposes, too detailed. A class diagram is a more abstract representation of the structure of a program. Instead of showing individual objects, it shows classes and the relationships between them.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              665.54296875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              665.54296875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              701.0
