              "id": "/page/195/ListItem/4",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li> Objects in one class might contain references to objects in another class. For example, each Rectangle contains a reference to a Point, and each Deck contains references to many Cards. This kind of relationship is called <b>HAS-A</b>, as in, \"a Rectangle has a Point.\"</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  100.0,
                  223.5234375
                ],
                [
                  483.50390625,
                  223.5234375
                ],
                [
                  483.50390625,
                  272.0
                ],
                [
                  100.0,
                  272.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/194/SectionHeader/10"
              },
              "images": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "/page/195/ListItem/5",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li> One class might inherit from another. This relationship is called <b>IS-A</b>, as in, \"a Hand is a kind of a Deck.\"</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  100.0,
                  279.791015625
                ],
                [
                  483.802734375,
                  279.791015625
                ],
                [
                  483.802734375,
                  304.0
                ],
                [
                  100.0,
                  304.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/194/SectionHeader/10"
              },
              "images": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "/page/195/ListItem/6",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li> One class might depend on another in the sense that changes in one class would require changes in the other.</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  100.0,
                  312.275390625
                ],
                [
                  483.802734375,
                  312.275390625
                ],
                [
                  483.802734375,
                  336.0
                ],
                [
                  100.0,
                  336.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/194/SectionHeader/10"
              },
              "images": {}
            }
          ],
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/194/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": null
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/195/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>A <b>class diagram</b> is a graphical representation of these relationships. For example, Figure 18.2 shows the relationships between Card, Deck and Hand.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              347.853515625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              347.853515625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              371.056640625
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              371.056640625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/194/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/195/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The arrow with a hollow triangle head represents an IS-A relationship; in this case it indicates that Hand inherits from Deck.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              378.984375
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              378.984375
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              403.0
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              403.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/194/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/195/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The standard arrow head represents a HAS-A relationship; in this case a Deck has references to Card objects.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              411.46875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              411.46875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              435.0
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              435.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/194/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/195/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The star (*) near the arrow head is a <b>multiplicity</b>; it indicates how many Cards a Deck has. A multiplicity can be a simple number, like 52, a range, like 5..7 or a star, which indicates that a Deck can have any number of Cards.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              443.56640625
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              443.56640625
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              479.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              479.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/194/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/195/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>A more detailed diagram might show that a Deck actually contains a <i>list</i> of Cards, but built-in types like list and dict are usually not included in class diagrams. <b>Exercise 18.4.</b> <i>Read</i> TurtleWorld.py, World.py and Gui.py <i>and draw a class diagram that</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              487.65234375
            ],
            [
              484.69921875,
              487.65234375
            ],
            [
              484.69921875,
              524.00390625
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              524.00390625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/194/SectionHeader/10"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/195/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>shows the relationships among the classes defined there.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              523.6171875
            ],
            [
              308.0,
              523.6171875
            ],
            [
              308.0,
              535.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              535.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>18.9 Debugging</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.68896484375,
              562.67578125
            ],
            [
              200.513671875,
              562.67578125
            ],
            [
              200.513671875,
              579.0
            ],
            [
              85.68896484375,
              579.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/195/Text/14",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Inheritance can make debugging a challenge because when you invoke a method on an object, you might not know which method will be invoked.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              588.5859375
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              588.5859375
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              613.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              613.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/195/Text/15",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Suppose you are writing a function that works with Hand objects. You would like it to work with all kinds of Hands, like PokerHands, BridgeHands, etc. If you invoke a method like shuffle, you might get the one defined in Deck, but if any of the subclasses override this method, you'll get that version instead.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              621.45703125
            ],
            [
              484.69921875,
              621.45703125
            ],
            [
              484.69921875,
              669.0
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              669.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/195/Text/16",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p class=\"has-continuation\">Any time you are unsure about the flow of execution through your program, the simplest solution is to add print statements at the beginning of the relevant methods. If</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              677.53125
            ],
            [
              485.595703125,
              677.53125
            ],
            [
              485.595703125,
              701.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              701.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        }
      ],
      "section_hierarchy": {
        "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
        "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
      },
      "images": null
    },
    {
      "id": "/page/196/Page/195",
      "block_type": "Page",
      "html": "<content-ref src='/page/196/PageHeader/0'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/PageHeader/14'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Text/1'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Text/2'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Code/3'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Text/16'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Text/4'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Text/15'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Text/5'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Text/6'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Text/17'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/SectionHeader/7'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Text/8'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Text/9'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Text/10'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Code/11'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Text/12'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/196/Text/13'></content-ref>",
      "polygon": [
        [
          0.0,
          0.0
        ],
        [
          612.0,
          0.0
        ],
        [
          612.0,
          792.0
        ],
        [
          0.0,
          792.0
        ]
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "/page/196/PageHeader/0",
          "block_type": "PageHeader",
          "html": "<p><b>18.10. Data encapsulation 175</b></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.0478515625,
              61.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              61.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              71.0
            ],
            [
              128.0478515625,
              71.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/PageHeader/14",
          "block_type": "PageHeader",
          "html": "<p></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              510.099609375,
              60.908203125
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              60.908203125
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              70.2861328125
            ],
            [
              510.099609375,
              70.2861328125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Text/1",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Deck.shuffle prints a message that says something like Running Deck.shuffle, then as the program runs it traces the flow of execution.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              89.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              89.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              111.0
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              111.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>As an alternative, you could use this function, which takes an object and a method name (as a string) and returns the class that provides the definition of the method:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              119.8828125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              119.8828125
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              144.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              144.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Code/3",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>def find_defining_class(obj, meth_name):\n    for ty in type(obj).mro():\n        if meth_name in ty.__dict__:\n            return ty\nHere's an example:\n&gt;&gt;&gt; hand = Hand()\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print find_defining_class(hand, 'shuffle&lt;class 'Card.Deck'&gt;</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.01904296875,
              150.0
            ],
            [
              360.0,
              150.0
            ],
            [
              360.0,
              257.5546875
            ],
            [
              129.01904296875,
              257.5546875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Text/16",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>So the shuffle method for this Hand is the one in Deck.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              130.0,
              260.0
            ],
            [
              375.0,
              260.0
            ],
            [
              375.0,
              271.0
            ],
            [
              130.0,
              271.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>find_defining_class uses the mro method to get the list of class objects (types) that will</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              271.08984375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              271.08984375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              292.359375
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              292.359375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Text/15",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>')</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              360.0,
              218.0
            ],
            [
              370.0,
              218.0
            ],
            [
              370.0,
              242.0
            ],
            [
              360.0,
              242.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Here's a program design suggestion: whenever you override a method, the interface of the new method should be the same as the old. It should take the same parameters, return the same type, and obey the same preconditions and postconditions. If you obey this rule, you will find that any function designed to work with an instance of a superclass, like a Deck, will also work with instances of subclasses like a Hand or PokerHand.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              127.7490234375,
              309.955078125
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              309.955078125
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              372.0
            ],
            [
              127.7490234375,
              372.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>If you violate this rule, your code will collapse like (sorry) a house of cards.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              382.271484375
            ],
            [
              460.79296875,
              382.271484375
            ],
            [
              460.79296875,
              393.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              393.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/195/SectionHeader/13"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Text/17",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>be searched for methods. \"MRO\" stands for \"method resolution order.\"</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              130.0,
              293.0
            ],
            [
              445.0,
              293.0
            ],
            [
              445.0,
              303.0
            ],
            [
              130.0,
              303.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/196/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/SectionHeader/7",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>18.10 Data encapsulation</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.61669921875,
              421.91015625
            ],
            [
              303.0,
              421.91015625
            ],
            [
              303.0,
              437.0
            ],
            [
              129.61669921875,
              437.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/196/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Chapter 16 demonstrates a development plan we might call \"object-oriented design.\" We identified objects we needed—Time, Point and Rectangle—and defined classes to represent them. In each case there is an obvious correspondence between the object and some entity in the real world (or at least a mathematical world).</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.5419921875,
              449.3671875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              449.3671875
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              497.0
            ],
            [
              129.5419921875,
              497.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/196/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>But sometimes it is less obvious what objects you need and how they should interact. In that case you need a different development plan. In the same way that we discovered function interfaces by encapsulation and generalization, we can discover class interfaces by <b>data encapsulation</b>.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              507.0
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              507.0
            ],
            [
              526.236328125,
              554.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              554.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/196/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Markov analysis, from Section 13.8, provides a good example. If you download my code from http://thinkpython.com/code/markov.py, you'll see that it uses two global variables—suffix_map and prefix—that are read and written from several functions.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              564.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              564.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              599.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              599.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/196/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Code/11",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>suffix_map = {}\nprefix = ()</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              604.828125
            ],
            [
              208.0,
              604.828125
            ],
            [
              208.0,
              627.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              627.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/196/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Because these variables are global we can only run one analysis at a time. If we read two texts, their prefixes and suffixes would be added to the same data structures (which makes for some interesting generated text).</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              634.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              634.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              668.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              668.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/188/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/196/SectionHeader/7"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/196/Text/13",
