          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/207/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/209/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>You can use the Draggable class to add drag-and-drop capability to an existing item. For example, here is a modified version of make_circle that uses circle to create an Item and Draggable to make it draggable:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              241.3125
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              241.3125
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              277.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              277.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/207/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/209/Code/7",
          "block_type": "Code",
          "html": "<pre>def make_circle(event):\n    pos = ca.canvas_coords([event.x, event.y])\n    item = ca.circle(pos, 5, fill='red')\n    item = Draggable(item)</pre>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.09130859375,
              282.3046875
            ],
            [
              327.0,
              282.3046875
            ],
            [
              327.0,
              330.64453125
            ],
            [
              85.09130859375,
              330.64453125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/207/SectionHeader/9"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/209/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>This example demonstrates one of the benefits of inheritance: you can modify the capabilities of a parent class without modifying its definition. This is particularly useful if you want to change behavior defined in a module you did not write.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              334.8984375
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              334.8984375
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              371.0
            ],
            [
