          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/211/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 19.4.</b> <i>For this exercise, you will write an image viewer. Here is a simple example:</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              112.921875
            ],
            [
              451.529296875,
              111.375
            ],
            [
              451.529296875,
              124.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              124.716796875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/211/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>g = Gui() canvas = g.ca(width=300) photo = PhotoImage(file='danger.gif') canvas.image([0,0], image=photo) g.mainloop()</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              84.79248046875,
              130.2275390625
            ],
            [
              280.0,
              128.6806640625
            ],
            [
              280.0,
              190.1689453125
            ],
            [
              84.79248046875,
              191.7158203125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/211/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>PhotoImage <i>reads a file and returns a</i> PhotoImage <i>object that Tkinter can display.</i> Canvas.image <i>puts the image on the canvas, centered on the given coordinates. You can also put images on labels,</i> <i>buttons, and some other widgets:</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.3154296875,
              196.06640625
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              196.06640625
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              232.03125
            ],
            [
              85.3154296875,
              232.03125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/211/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>g.la(image=photo) g.bu(image=photo)</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              238.21875
            ],
            [
              176.4580078125,
              238.21875
            ],
            [
              176.4580078125,
              262.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              262.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/211/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>PhotoImage can only handle a few image formats, like GIF and PPM, but we can use the Python</i> <i>Imaging Library (PIL) to read other files.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.3154296875,
              267.609375
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              267.609375
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              291.19921875
            ],
            [
              85.3154296875,
              291.19921875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/211/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>The name of the PIL module is</i> Image<i>, but Tkinter defines an object with the same name. To avoid</i> <i>the conflict, you can use</i> import...as <i>like this:</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              300.48046875
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              300.48046875
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              325.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              325.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/211/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>import Image as PIL import ImageTk</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.39013671875,
              331.41796875
            ],
            [
              186.0,
              331.41796875
            ],
            [
              186.0,
              355.0
            ],
            [
              85.39013671875,
              355.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/211/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>The first line imports</i> Image <i>and gives it the local name</i> PIL<i>. The second line imports</i> ImageTk, <i>which can translate a PIL image into a Tkinter PhotoImage. Here's an example:</i> ''</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              361.388671875
            ],
            [
              484.69921875,
              361.388671875
            ],
            [
              484.69921875,
              388.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              388.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/211/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>image = PIL.open(allen.png) photo2 = ImageTk.PhotoImage(image) g.la(image=photo2)</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.68896484375,
              391.552734375
            ],
            [
              264.0,
              391.552734375
            ],
            [
              264.0,
              427.32421875
            ],
            [
              85.68896484375,
              427.32421875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/211/ListGroup/233",
          "block_type": "ListGroup",
          "html": "<p><ul><content-ref src='/page/211/ListItem/11'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/211/ListItem/12'></content-ref></ul></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              98.1650390625,
              440.47265625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              440.47265625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              524.0
            ],
            [
              98.1650390625,
              524.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "id": "/page/211/ListItem/11",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li><i>1. Download</i> image_demo.py, danger.gif and allen.png <i>from</i> http: // thinkpython. com/ code <i>. Run</i> image_demo.py<i>. You might have to install</i> PIL and ImageTk<i>. They</i> <i>are probably in your software repository, but if not you can get them from</i> http: // pythonware. com/ products/ pil .</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  98.1650390625,
                  440.47265625
                ],
                [
                  483.50390625,
                  440.47265625
                ],
                [
                  483.50390625,
                  489.19921875
                ],
                [
                  98.1650390625,
                  489.19921875
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
              },
              "images": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "/page/211/ListItem/12",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li><i>2. In</i> image_demo.py <i>change the name of the second PhotoImage from</i> photo2 to photo and <i>run the program again. You should see the second PhotoImage but not the first.</i></li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  98.1650390625,
                  500.02734375
                ],
                [
                  483.50390625,
                  500.02734375
                ],
                [
                  483.50390625,
                  524.0
                ],
                [
                  98.1650390625,
                  524.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
              },
              "images": {}
            }
          ],
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": null
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/211/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>The problem is that when you reassign</i> photo <i>it overwrites the reference to the first PhotoIm</i><i>age, which then disappears. The same thing happens if you assign a PhotoImage to a local</i> <i>variable; it disappears when the function ends.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              110.267578125,
              530.19140625
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              530.19140625
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              565.0
            ],
            [
              110.267578125,
              565.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/211/Text/14",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>To avoid this problem, you have to store a reference to each PhotoImage you want to keep. You</i> <i>can use a global variable, or store PhotoImages in a data structure or as an attribute of an</i> <i>object.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              110.56640625,
              571.5703125
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              571.5703125
            ],
            [
              483.802734375,
              607.1484375
            ],
            [
              110.56640625,
              607.1484375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/211/Text/15",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>This behavior can be frustrating, which is why I am warning you (and why the example image</i> <i>says \"Danger!\").</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              109.96875,
              612.5625
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              612.5625
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              637.0
            ],
            [
              109.96875,
              637.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/211/ListItem/16",
          "block_type": "ListItem",
          "html": "<li><i>3. Starting with this example, write a program that takes the name of a directory and loops</i> <i>through all the files, displaying any files that PIL recognizes as images. You can use a</i> try <i>statement to catch the files PIL doesn't recognize.</i></li>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              98.015625,
              646.98046875
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              646.98046875
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              683.33203125
            ],
            [
              98.015625,
              683.33203125
