        },
        {
          "id": "/page/212/ListItem/1",
          "block_type": "ListItem",
          "html": "<li><i>4. PIL provides a variety of methods for manipulating images. You can read about them at</i> http: // pythonware. com/ library/ pil/ handbook <i>. As a challenge, choose a few of</i> <i>these methods and provide a GUI for applying them to images.</i></li>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              140.748046875,
              87.6884765625
            ],
            [
              527.431640625,
              87.6884765625
            ],
            [
              527.431640625,
              123.0
            ],
            [
              140.748046875,
              123.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/212/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>Solution:</i> http: // thinkpython. com/ code/ ImageBrowser. py .</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.42138671875,
              136.6083984375
            ],
            [
              409.0,
              136.6083984375
            ],
            [
              409.0,
              147.0
            ],
            [
              128.42138671875,
              147.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/212/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise 19.5.</b> <i>A vector graphics editor is a program that allows users to draw and edit shapes on</i> <i>the screen and generate output files in vector graphics formats like Postscript and SVG.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              148.88671875
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              148.88671875
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              172.0
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              172.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/212/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>Write a simple vector graphics editor using Tkinter. At a minimum, it should allow users to draw</i> <i>lines, circles and rectangles, and it should use</i> Canvas.dump <i>to generate a Postscript description of</i> <i>the contents of the Canvas.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              180.984375
            ],
            [
              528.029296875,
              180.984375
            ],
            [
              528.029296875,
              216.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              216.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/212/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>As a challenge, you could allow users to select and resize items on the Canvas.</i> <b>Exercise 19.6.</b> <i>Use Tkinter to write a basic web browser. It should have a Text widget where the</i> <i>user can enter a URL and a Canvas to display the contents of the page.</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              224.103515625
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              224.103515625
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              261.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              261.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/200/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/211/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/212/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><i>You can use the</i> urllib <i>module to download files (see Exercise 14.6) and the</i> HTMLParser <i>module</i> <i>to parse the HTML tags (see</i> http: // docs. python. org/ 2/ library/ htmlparser. html ).</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.09375,
              269.736328125
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              269.736328125
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              293.0
            ],
            [
              129.09375,
              293.0
