              },
              "images": {}
            }
          ],
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/214/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/221/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": null
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/221/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>If you find yourself suffering from any of these symptoms, get up and go for a walk. When you are calm, think about the program. What is it doing? What are some possible causes of that behavior? When was the last time you had a working program, and what did you do next?</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              233.578125
            ],
            [
              484.69921875,
              233.578125
            ],
            [
              484.69921875,
              282.0
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              282.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/214/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/221/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/221/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Sometimes it just takes time to find a bug. I often find bugs when I am away from the computer and let my mind wander. Some of the best places to find bugs are trains, showers, and in bed, just before you fall asleep.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              290.42578125
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              290.42578125
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              327.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              327.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/214/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/221/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/221/SectionHeader/8",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>A.3.5 No, I really need help.</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              351.333984375
            ],
            [
              247.728515625,
              351.333984375
            ],
            [
              247.728515625,
              365.642578125
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              365.642578125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/214/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/221/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/221/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>It happens. Even the best programmers occasionally get stuck. Sometimes you work on a program so long that you can't see the error. A fresh pair of eyes is just the thing.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              373.95703125
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              373.95703125
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              398.3203125
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              398.3203125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/214/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/221/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/221/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Before you bring someone else in, make sure you are prepared. Your program should be as simple as possible, and you should be working on the smallest input that causes the error. You should have print statements in the appropriate places (and the output they produce should be comprehensible). You should understand the problem well enough to describe it concisely.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              406.0546875
            ],
            [
              484.69921875,
              406.0546875
            ],
            [
              484.69921875,
              467.0
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              467.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/214/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/221/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/221/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>When you bring someone in to help, be sure to give them the information they need:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              474.50390625
            ],
            [
              460.1953125,
              474.50390625
            ],
            [
              460.1953125,
              487.0
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              487.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/214/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/221/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/221/ListGroup/120",
          "block_type": "ListGroup",
          "html": "<p><ul><content-ref src='/page/221/ListItem/12'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/221/ListItem/13'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/221/ListItem/14'></content-ref></ul></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              100.0,
              498.48046875
            ],
            [
              485.296875,
              498.48046875
            ],
            [
              485.296875,
              563.0
            ],
            [
              100.0,
              563.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "id": "/page/221/ListItem/12",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li> If there is an error message, what is it and what part of the program does it indicate?</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  100.0,
                  498.48046875
                ],
                [
                  483.50390625,
                  498.48046875
                ],
                [
                  483.50390625,
                  511.0
                ],
                [
                  100.0,
                  511.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/214/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/221/SectionHeader/8"
              },
              "images": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "/page/221/ListItem/13",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li> What was the last thing you did before this error occurred? What were the last lines of code that you wrote, or what is the new test case that fails?</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  100.0,
                  517.81640625
                ],
                [
                  485.296875,
                  517.81640625
                ],
                [
                  485.296875,
                  543.33984375
                ],
                [
                  100.0,
                  543.33984375
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/214/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/221/SectionHeader/8"
              },
              "images": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "/page/221/ListItem/14",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li> What have you tried so far, and what have you learned?</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  100.0,
                  550.6875
                ],
                [
                  360.984375,
                  550.6875
                ],
                [
                  360.984375,
                  563.0
                ],
                [
                  100.0,
                  563.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/214/SectionHeader/1",
                "3": "/page/221/SectionHeader/8"
              },
              "images": {}
            }
          ],
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/214/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/221/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": null
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/221/Text/15",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>When you find the bug, take a second to think about what you could have done to find it faster. Next time you see something similar, you will be able to find the bug more quickly.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              575.82421875
            ],
            [
              484.400390625,
              575.82421875
            ],
            [
              484.400390625,
              600.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              600.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/214/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/221/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/221/Text/16",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>Remember, the goal is not just to make the program work. The goal is to learn how to make the program work.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              609.08203125
            ],
            [
              484.69921875,
              607.53515625
            ],
            [
              484.69921875,
              632.0
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              633.05859375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/214/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/221/SectionHeader/8"
          },
          "images": {}
        }
      ],
      "section_hierarchy": {
        "1": "/page/214/SectionHeader/1",
        "3": "/page/222/SectionHeader/0"
      },
      "images": null
    },
    {
      "id": "/page/222/Page/125",
      "block_type": "Page",
      "html": "<content-ref src='/page/222/SectionHeader/0'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/222/SectionHeader/1'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/222/Text/2'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/222/Text/3'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/222/Text/4'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/222/Text/5'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/222/Text/6'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/222/Text/7'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/222/ListGroup/125'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/222/Footnote/10'></content-ref>",
      "polygon": [
        [
          0.0,
          0.0
        ],
        [
          612.0,
          0.0
        ],
        [
          612.0,
          792.0
        ],
        [
          0.0,
          792.0
        ]
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "/page/222/SectionHeader/0",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>Appendix B</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.392578125,
              163.2919921875
            ],
            [
              243.0,
              163.2919921875
            ],
            [
              243.0,
              185.1416015625
            ],
            [
              129.392578125,
              185.1416015625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h1><b>Analysis of Algorithms</b></h1>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.31787109375,
              217.916015625
            ],
            [
              394.751953125,
              217.916015625
            ],
            [
              394.751953125,
              244.0
            ],
            [
              129.31787109375,
              244.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/222/Text/2",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>This appendix is an edited excerpt from <i>Think Complexity</i>, by Allen B. Downey, also published by O'Reilly Media (2011). When you are done with this book, you might want to move on to that one.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              154.79296875,
              289.0
            ],
            [
              502.03125,
              289.0
            ],
            [
              502.03125,
              323.0
            ],
            [
              154.79296875,
              323.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/222/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Analysis of algorithms</b> is a branch of computer science that studies the performance of algorithms, especially their run time and space requirements. See http://en.wikipedia. org/wiki/Analysis_of_algorithms.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.09375,
              336.4453125
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              336.4453125
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              372.0
            ],
            [
              129.09375,
              372.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/222/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The practical goal of algorithm analysis is to predict the performance of different algorithms in order to guide design decisions.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              381.884765625
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              381.884765625
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              405.0
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              405.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/222/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>During the 2008 United States Presidential Campaign, candidate Barack Obama was asked to perform an impromptu analysis when he visited Google. Chief executive Eric Schmidt jokingly asked him for \"the most efficient way to sort a million 32-bit integers.\" Obama had apparently been tipped off, because he quickly replied, \"I think the bubble sort would be the wrong way to go.\" See http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k4RRi_ntQc8.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.197265625,
              414.17578125
            ],
            [
              527.431640625,
              414.17578125
            ],
            [
              527.431640625,
              474.0
            ],
            [
              128.197265625,
              474.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/222/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>This is true: bubble sort is conceptually simple but slow for large datasets. The answer Schmidt was probably looking for is \"radix sort\" (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ Radix_sort) 1 .</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              484.0
            ],
            [
              527.73046875,
              484.0
            ],
            [
              527.73046875,
              518.203125
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              518.203125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/222/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The goal of algorithm analysis is to make meaningful comparisons between algorithms, but there are some problems:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.197265625,
              527.484375
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              527.484375
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              551.0
            ],
            [
              128.197265625,
              551.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/222/ListGroup/125",
          "block_type": "ListGroup",
          "html": "<p><ul><content-ref src='/page/222/ListItem/8'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/222/ListItem/9'></content-ref></ul></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              143.0,
              564.22265625
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              564.22265625
            ],
            [
              527.1328125,
              644.0
            ],
            [
              143.0,
              644.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "id": "/page/222/ListItem/8",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li> The relative performance of the algorithms might depend on characteristics of the hardware, so one algorithm might be faster on Machine A, another on Machine B. The general solution to this problem is to specify a <b>machine model</b> and analyze the number of steps, or operations, an algorithm requires under a given model.</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  143.0,
                  564.22265625
                ],
                [
                  527.1328125,
                  564.22265625
                ],
                [
                  527.1328125,
                  611.0
                ],
                [
                  143.0,
                  611.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
              },
              "images": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "/page/222/ListItem/9",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li> Relative performance might depend on the details of the dataset. For example, some sorting algorithms run faster if the data are already partially sorted; other algorithms</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  143.0,
                  621.0703125
                ],
                [
                  526.53515625,
                  621.0703125
                ],
                [
                  526.53515625,
                  644.0
                ],
                [
                  143.0,
                  644.0
                ]
              ],
              "children": null,
              "section_hierarchy": {
                "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
              },
              "images": {}
            }
          ],
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": null
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/222/Footnote/10",
          "block_type": "Footnote",
          "html": "<p><sup>1 </sup>But if you get a question like this in an interview, I think a better answer is, \"The fastest way to sort a million integers is to use whatever sort function is provided by the language I'm using. Its performance is good enough for the vast majority of applications, but if it turned out that my application was too slow, I would use a profiler to see where the time was being spent. If it looked like a faster sort algorithm would have a significant effect on performance, then I would look around for a good implementation of radix sort.\"</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.3466796875,
              653.0
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              653.0
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              700.734375
            ],
            [
              128.3466796875,
              700.734375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        }
      ],
      "section_hierarchy": {
        "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
      },
      "images": null
    },
    {
      "id": "/page/223/Page/261",
      "block_type": "Page",
      "html": "<content-ref src='/page/223/PageHeader/0'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/PageHeader/15'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/Text/1'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/ListItem/2'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/Text/3'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/Text/4'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/SectionHeader/5'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/TextInlineMath/6'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/Text/7'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/Table/8'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/Text/9'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/Text/10'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/Text/11'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/Text/12'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/Text/13'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/223/Text/14'></content-ref>",
      "polygon": [
        [
          0.0,
          0.0
        ],
        [
          612.0,
          0.0
        ],
        [
          612.0,
          792.0
        ],
        [
          0.0,
          792.0
        ]
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "/page/223/PageHeader/0",
          "block_type": "PageHeader",
          "html": "<p><b>202 Appendix B. Analysis of Algorithms</b></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              86.0,
              60.521484375
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              60.521484375
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              71.0
            ],
            [
              86.0,
              71.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/PageHeader/15",
          "block_type": "PageHeader",
          "html": "<p></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.3154296875,
              60.2314453125
            ],
            [
              101.1533203125,
              60.2314453125
            ],
            [
              101.1533203125,
              70.2861328125
            ],
            [
              85.3154296875,
              70.2861328125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/Text/1",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>run slower in this case. A common way to avoid this problem is to analyze the <b>worst</b> <b>case</b> scenario. It is sometimes useful to analyze average case performance, but that's usually harder, and it might not be obvious what set of cases to average over.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              110.1181640625,
              87.8818359375
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              87.8818359375
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              123.0
            ],
            [
              110.1181640625,
              123.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/ListItem/2",
          "block_type": "ListItem",
          "html": "<li> Relative performance also depends on the size of the problem. A sorting algorithm that is fast for small lists might be slow for long lists. The usual solution to this problem is to express run time (or number of operations) as a function of problem size, and to compare the functions <b>asymptotically</b> as the problem size increases.</li>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              100.0,
              131.7744140625
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              131.7744140625
            ],
            [
              482.90625,
              179.0
            ],
            [
              100.0,
              179.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The good thing about this kind of comparison that it lends itself to simple classification of algorithms. For example, if I know that the run time of Algorithm A tends to be proportional to the size of the input, n, and Algorithm B tends to be proportional to n 2 , then I expect A to be faster than B for large values of n.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              187.751953125
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              187.751953125
            ],
            [
              483.205078125,
              236.091796875
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              236.091796875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>This kind of analysis comes with some caveats, but we'll get to that later.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.9130859375,
              244.01953125
            ],
            [
              406.40625,
              244.01953125
            ],
            [
              406.40625,
              255.0
            ],
            [
              85.9130859375,
              255.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h3><b>B.1 Order of growth</b></h3>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.3154296875,
              280.564453125
            ],
            [
              229.6494140625,
              280.564453125
            ],
            [
              229.6494140625,
              296.033203125
            ],
            [
              85.3154296875,
              296.033203125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/TextInlineMath/6",
          "block_type": "TextInlineMath",
          "html": "<p>Suppose you have analyzed two algorithms and expressed their run times in terms of the size of the input: Algorithm A takes 100n + 1 steps to solve a problem with size n; Algorithm B takes n 2 + n + 1 steps.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              306.66796875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              306.66796875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              342.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              342.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/Text/7",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The following table shows the run time of these algorithms for different problem sizes:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              349.59375
            ],
            [
              467.666015625,
              349.59375
            ],
            [
              467.666015625,
              361.0
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              361.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/Table/8",
          "block_type": "Table",
          "html": "<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th>Input  </th><th>Run time of  </th><th>Run time of  </th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>size   </td><td>Algorithm A  </td><td>Algorithm B  </td></tr>\n<tr><td>10     </td><td>1 001        </td><td>111          </td></tr>\n<tr><td>100    </td><td>10 001       </td><td>10 101       </td></tr>\n<tr><td>1 000  </td><td>100 001      </td><td>1 001 001    </td></tr>\n<tr><td>10 000 </td><td>1 000 001    </td><td>&gt;  1010      </td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.166015625,
              367.76953125
            ],
            [
              261.474609375,
              367.76953125
            ],
            [
              261.474609375,
              444.33984375
            ],
            [
              85.166015625,
              444.33984375
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>At n = 10, Algorithm A looks pretty bad; it takes almost 10 times longer than Algorithm B. But for n = 100 they are about the same, and for larger values A is much better.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.3154296875,
              449.75390625
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              449.75390625
            ],
            [
              482.30859375,
              473.0
            ],
            [
              85.3154296875,
              473.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/Text/10",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The fundamental reason is that for large values of n, any function that contains an n 2 term will grow faster than a function whose leading term is n. The <b>leading term</b> is the term with the highest exponent.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.3154296875,
              480.0
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              480.0
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              516.0
            ],
            [
              85.3154296875,
              516.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/Text/11",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>For Algorithm A, the leading term has a large coefficient, 100, which is why B does better than A for small n. But regardless of the coefficients, there will always be some value of n where an2 <i>&gt;</i> bn.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.46484375,
              523.6171875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              523.6171875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              559.1953125
            ],
            [
              85.46484375,
              559.1953125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/Text/12",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The same argument applies to the non-leading terms. Even if the run time of Algorithm A were n + 1000000, it would still be better than Algorithm B for sufficiently large n.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              566.9296875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              566.9296875
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              590.1328125
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              590.1328125
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/Text/13",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>In general, we expect an algorithm with a smaller leading term to be a better algorithm for large problems, but for smaller problems, there may be a <b>crossover point</b> where another algorithm is better. The location of the crossover point depends on the details of the algorithms, the inputs, and the hardware, so it is usually ignored for purposes of algorithmic analysis. But that doesn't mean you can forget about it.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.763671875,
              598.25390625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              598.25390625
            ],
            [
              483.50390625,
              658.0
            ],
            [
              85.763671875,
              658.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/223/Text/14",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>If two algorithms have the same leading order term, it is hard to say which is better; again, the answer depends on the details. So for algorithmic analysis, functions with the same leading term are considered equivalent, even if they have different coefficients.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              85.6142578125,
              665.9296875
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              665.9296875
            ],
            [
              484.1015625,
              701.0
            ],
            [
              85.6142578125,
              701.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        }
      ],
      "section_hierarchy": {
        "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
        "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
      },
      "images": null
    },
    {
      "id": "/page/224/Page/395",
      "block_type": "Page",
      "html": "<content-ref src='/page/224/PageHeader/0'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/PageHeader/15'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/Text/1'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/TextInlineMath/2'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/Text/3'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/Table/4'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/Text/5'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/Text/6'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/ListGroup/395'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/Text/13'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/Text/14'></content-ref>",
      "polygon": [
        [
          0.0,
          0.0
        ],
        [
          612.0,
          0.0
        ],
        [
          612.0,
          792.0
        ],
        [
          0.0,
          792.0
        ]
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "/page/224/PageHeader/0",
          "block_type": "PageHeader",
          "html": "<p><b>B.1. Order of growth 203</b></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.49609375,
              61.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              61.0
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              71.0
            ],
            [
              128.49609375,
              71.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/224/PageHeader/15",
          "block_type": "PageHeader",
          "html": "<p></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              510.099609375,
              60.521484375
            ],
            [
              525.638671875,
              60.521484375
            ],
            [
              525.638671875,
              70.6728515625
            ],
            [
              510.099609375,
              70.6728515625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/224/Text/1",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>An <b>order of growth</b> is a set of functions whose asymptotic growth behavior is considered equivalent. For example, 2n, 100n and n + 1 belong to the same order of growth, which is written O(n) in <b>Big-Oh notation</b> and often called <b>linear</b> because every function in the set grows linearly with n.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              88.2685546875
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              88.2685546875
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              135.0
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              135.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/224/TextInlineMath/2",
          "block_type": "TextInlineMath",
          "html": "<p>All functions with the leading term n 2 belong to O(n 2 ); they are <b>quadratic</b>, which is a fancy word for functions with the leading term n 2 .</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              144.0
            ],
            [
              527.431640625,
              144.0
            ],
            [
              527.431640625,
              168.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              168.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/224/Text/3",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>The following table shows some of the orders of growth that appear most commonly in algorithmic analysis, in increasing order of badness.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.6455078125,
              178.1806640625
            ],
            [
              527.73046875,
              178.1806640625
            ],
            [
              527.73046875,
              201.48046875
            ],
            [
              128.6455078125,
              201.48046875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/224/Table/4",
          "block_type": "Table",
          "html": "<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th>Order of  </th><th> </th><th>Name                   </th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>growth    </td><td> </td><td> </td></tr>\n<tr><td>O(1)      </td><td> </td><td>constant               </td></tr>\n<tr><td>O(logb    </td><td>n) </td><td>logarithmic (for any b)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>O(n)      </td><td> </td><td>linear                 </td></tr>\n<tr><td>O(n  logb </td><td>n) </td><td>“en log en”            </td></tr>\n<tr><td>O(n       </td><td>2 )</td><td>quadratic              </td></tr>\n<tr><td>O(n       </td><td>3 )</td><td>cubic                  </td></tr>\n<tr><td>O(c       </td><td>n )</td><td>exponential (for any c)</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              129.91552734375,
              208.828125
            ],
            [
              302.115234375,
              208.828125
            ],
            [
              302.115234375,
              323.296875
            ],
            [
              129.91552734375,
              323.296875
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/224/Text/5",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p>For the logarithmic terms, the base of the logarithm doesn't matter; changing bases is the equivalent of multiplying by a constant, which doesn't change the order of growth. Similarly, all exponential functions belong to the same order of growth regardless of the base of the exponent. Exponential functions grow very quickly, so exponential algorithms are only useful for small problems.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.9443359375,
              331.41796875
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              331.41796875
            ],
            [
              526.53515625,
              390.97265625
            ],
            [
              128.9443359375,
              390.97265625
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/224/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p><b>Exercise B.1.</b> <i>Read the Wikipedia page on Big-Oh notation at</i> http: // en. wikipedia. org/ wiki/ Big_ O_ notation <i>and answer the following questions:</i></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              128.794921875,
              391.939453125
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              391.939453125
            ],
            [
              526.833984375,
              415.0
            ],
            [
              128.794921875,
              415.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "1": "/page/222/SectionHeader/1",
            "3": "/page/223/SectionHeader/5"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/224/ListGroup/395",
          "block_type": "ListGroup",
          "html": "<p><ul><content-ref src='/page/224/ListItem/7'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/ListItem/8'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/ListItem/9'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/ListItem/10'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/ListItem/11'></content-ref><content-ref src='/page/224/ListItem/12'></content-ref></ul></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              140.8974609375,
              426.9375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              426.9375
            ],
            [
              526.0,
              571.0
            ],
            [
              140.8974609375,
              571.0
            ]
          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "id": "/page/224/ListItem/7",
              "block_type": "ListItem",
              "html": "<li><i>1. What is the order of growth of n</i>3 + n 2<i>? What about</i> 1000000n 3 + n 2<i>? What about n</i>3 + 1000000n 2?</li>",
              "polygon": [
                [
                  141.1962890625,
